starterCode:
// Scenario:
// An EC2 instance running your application needs to write files to an S3 bucket.
// A colleague proposes storing AWS access keys in the app config file on the instance.
//
// Question: What is the AWS best practice for granting EC2 permissions to access S3?
// Why is the colleague's approach problematic?
// How would you implement the correct solution?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// BEST PRACTICE: Use an IAM Role attached to the EC2 instance (Instance Profile)
//
// HOW TO IMPLEMENT:
// - Create an IAM Role with a trust policy allowing EC2 to assume it (ec2.amazonaws.com)
// - Attach a permission policy granting s3:PutObject (and other needed S3 actions) to the role
// - Attach the IAM Role to the EC2 instance as an Instance Profile
// - The AWS SDK on the instance automatically retrieves temporary credentials from
//   the EC2 Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) at http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/
// - Credentials are automatically rotated by AWS — no manual rotation needed
//
// WHY ACCESS KEYS IN CONFIG IS BAD:
// - Long-term credentials that never expire (unless manually rotated)
// - Risk of accidental exposure via version control, logs, or instance snapshots
// - If compromised, attacker has persistent access until keys are manually revoked
// - Violates the principle of least privilege and AWS security best practices
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - IAM Roles for EC2 = Instance Profiles; temporary credentials auto-refreshed every ~6 hours
// - Never store long-term access keys on EC2 instances — this is a critical exam concept
// - The SDK credential provider chain checks Instance Metadata automatically (no code changes needed)
// - Trust policy defines WHO can assume the role; permission policy defines WHAT they can do
// - AWS will flag hardcoded credentials in code via IAM Access Analyzer and Trusted Advisor

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// 50 developers need identical permissions: S3 read access + Lambda invoke.
// New developers join regularly; some switch teams.
// Currently each developer has policies attached directly to their IAM User.
//
// Question: What is the MOST efficient way to manage permissions at scale?
// How does this approach simplify onboarding and offboarding?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: Use IAM Groups to manage permissions at scale
//
// IMPLEMENTATION:
// - Create IAM Groups that represent job functions or teams (e.g., 'BackendDevelopers')
// - Attach the required permission policies to the GROUP (not to individual users)
// - Add each IAM User to the appropriate group(s)
// - When a developer joins: create user → add to group → they inherit all group permissions
// - When a developer leaves: remove user or disable → group memberships auto-removed
// - When a developer switches teams: remove from old group, add to new group
// - When permissions change: update the group policy once — ALL 50 members get the change
//
// SCALING EXAMPLE:
// - Without groups: adding a new S3 bucket permission = 50 individual policy updates
// - With groups: adding a new S3 bucket permission = 1 group policy update
//
// IMPORTANT LIMITS TO KNOW:
// - A user can belong to up to 10 IAM groups
// - Maximum 300 IAM groups per account (soft limit)
// - Groups cannot be nested (no groups within groups)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - IAM Groups are for managing users — they are NOT identities and cannot assume roles
// - Groups cannot be referenced as a Principal in resource-based policies
// - Attach policies to groups, not individual users, for operational efficiency
// - Users inherit all permissions from all groups they belong to (plus their own inline policies)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// You are architecting a multi-account AWS organization.
// Your security team asks you to explain three IAM concepts:
// 1. IAM Identity Policies
// 2. Permission Boundaries
// 3. Service Control Policies (SCPs)
//
// Question: What is the purpose of each? When does each apply?
// Can a developer do something that SCPs forbid, even if their IAM policy allows it?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// 1. IAM IDENTITY POLICIES (managed or inline):
//    - WHAT: Policies attached to IAM Users, Groups, or Roles that GRANT permissions
//    - WHEN: Applied every time a principal makes an AWS API call
//    - SCOPE: Single account, specific identities
//    - Example: Allow s3:GetObject on arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*
//
// 2. PERMISSION BOUNDARIES:
//    - WHAT: An IAM managed policy set as the MAXIMUM permissions an identity can have
//    - WHEN: Used when delegating permission creation (e.g., allowing devs to create roles
//            but ensuring those roles can never exceed the developer's own permissions)
//    - SCOPE: Single identity (User or Role) — does NOT grant permissions, only limits them
//    - Effective permissions = Identity Policy AND Permission Boundary (intersection)
//    - Example: Dev has s3:* in identity policy but boundary only allows s3:GetObject
//              → Effective permission is only s3:GetObject
//
// 3. SERVICE CONTROL POLICIES (SCPs):
//    - WHAT: Policies in AWS Organizations that set MAXIMUM permissions for entire accounts
//    - WHEN: Applied before any IAM evaluation — organizational governance layer
//    - SCOPE: Entire AWS account or OU (Organizational Unit)
//    - SCPs do NOT grant permissions — they only restrict what CAN be allowed
//    - Even the account root user is subject to SCPs (except the management account)
//    - Example: SCP denies all actions outside us-east-1 and us-west-2
//              → No IAM policy in that account can allow actions in eu-west-1
//
// EFFECTIVE PERMISSIONS FORMULA:
//    Effective = SCP ∩ Permission Boundary ∩ Identity Policy (minus explicit Denies)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - SCPs + Permission Boundaries = guardrails (set maximums, do NOT grant access)
// - An explicit Deny in ANY policy ALWAYS overrides an Allow
// - SCPs apply to all principals in an account EXCEPT the management/master account
// - Permission Boundaries must be managed policies (not inline)
// - If no SCP is attached, full AWS permissions are available (default FullAWSAccess SCP)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Account A (dev) has a Lambda function.
// Account B (prod) has a DynamoDB table.
// Lambda must read DynamoDB data without storing long-term credentials.
//
// Question: How do you configure cross-account access using IAM?
// What needs to be configured in BOTH accounts?
// What AWS API call does the Lambda use at runtime?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: Cross-account IAM Role + STS AssumeRole
//
// STEP 1 — In Account B (DynamoDB account), create a cross-account IAM Role:
//   - Create IAM Role in Account B
//   - Trust Policy: Allow Account A's Lambda execution role to assume this role
//     Principal: arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/lambda-execution-role
//     Action: sts:AssumeRole
//   - Permission Policy: Grant dynamodb:GetItem, dynamodb:Query on the specific table
//
// STEP 2 — In Account A (Lambda account):
//   - Add to the Lambda's execution role policy:
//     Action: sts:AssumeRole
//     Resource: arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:role/cross-account-dynamo-role
//
// STEP 3 — In Lambda code at runtime:
//   - Call STS AssumeRole to get temporary credentials (AccessKeyId, SecretAccessKey, SessionToken)
//   - Use these temporary credentials to create a DynamoDB client targeting Account B
//   - Credentials are valid for 15 minutes to 1 hour (configurable)
//   - Must handle credential refresh for long-running processes
//
// TRUST RELATIONSHIP CONCEPT:
//   Account B's role says: "I TRUST Account A's Lambda role to assume me"
//   Account A's Lambda role says: "I have PERMISSION to assume Account B's role"
//   Both sides must agree for the cross-account access to work
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Cross-account access ALWAYS requires trust on BOTH sides
// - STS AssumeRole returns temporary credentials (never long-term keys)
// - The assumed role's permissions apply; the original role's permissions do NOT carry over
// - You can add ExternalId condition to prevent confused deputy attacks
// - Session duration: minimum 15 minutes, maximum 12 hours (default 1 hour)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Users have S3 permissions but must complete MFA before deleting objects.
// Regular operations (read, write) work without MFA.
// Only s3:DeleteObject should require MFA verification.
//
// Question: What IAM condition key enforces MFA requirement?
// How do you structure the policy to allow reads without MFA but block deletes without MFA?
// What does the condition actually check?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// CONDITION KEY: aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent
//
// POLICY APPROACH — Use a Deny statement with a Null/False condition:
// {
//   "Effect": "Deny",
//   "Action": ["s3:DeleteObject", "s3:DeleteBucket"],
//   "Resource": "*",
//   "Condition": {
//     "BoolIfExists": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "false"}
//   }
// }
//
// HOW IT WORKS:
// - aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = true → MFA was used during login
// - aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = false OR absent → no MFA used
// - BoolIfExists handles both cases: key present+false AND key absent
// - If user did NOT use MFA: condition matches → Deny fires → delete blocked
// - If user DID use MFA: condition does NOT match → Deny does not fire → delete allowed
//
// IMPORTANT NUANCES:
// - aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is ABSENT (not false) for assumed roles without MFA
// - Use 'Null' condition operator to check if key is absent:
//   {"Null": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"}} catches both false and absent
// - BoolIfExists is safest: treats missing key as 'false' for Bool comparisons
// - aws:MultiFactorAuthAge: checks how many seconds ago MFA was authenticated
//   Use for time-bounded MFA (e.g., require fresh MFA within last 3600 seconds)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Deny + Condition(MFA not present) is safer than Allow + Condition(MFA present)
// - BoolIfExists handles the case where the condition key doesn't exist in the request
// - aws:MultiFactorAuthAge lets you enforce recent MFA (not just 'ever used MFA')
// - MFA conditions work with IAM users; federated roles need careful handling

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Developer's identity-based policies:
// - Policy 1 (on user): Allow s3:* on *
// - Policy 2 (on user): Deny s3:DeleteObject on *
// - Policy 3 (on group): Allow s3:DeleteObject on arn:aws:s3:::app-bucket/*
//
// The developer gets AccessDenied when trying to delete.
//
// Question: Why is deletion denied? What is the IAM policy evaluation logic?
// What change would allow deletion?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// REASON FOR DENIAL: Explicit Deny always overrides any number of Allows
//
// IAM POLICY EVALUATION ORDER:
// 1. Start: DENY by default (implicit deny)
// 2. Evaluate all applicable policies (identity, resource, SCP, boundaries)
// 3. If ANY policy has an EXPLICIT DENY → request is DENIED immediately (full stop)
// 4. If no explicit deny AND at least one ALLOW → request is ALLOWED
// 5. If no explicit deny AND no allow → request is DENIED (implicit deny)
//
// IN THIS SCENARIO:
// - Policy 2 has Explicit Deny on s3:DeleteObject
// - Explicit Deny ALWAYS wins — no matter how many Allows exist
// - Policy 1 (Allow s3:*) and Policy 3 (Allow s3:DeleteObject on group) are BOTH ignored
// - The result: DENIED
//
// TO FIX: Remove or modify Policy 2's Deny statement
// - Option A: Remove Policy 2 entirely
// - Option B: Add a condition to Policy 2's Deny (e.g., only deny without MFA)
// - Option C: Scope Policy 2's Deny to a different resource (not the target bucket)
// - NOTE: You CANNOT add another Allow to override the explicit Deny
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Explicit Deny ALWAYS wins — this is the most important rule in IAM
// - An explicit Deny in one policy cannot be overridden by an Allow in another policy
// - Implicit Deny (absence of Allow) CAN be overridden by an Allow elsewhere
// - 'Deny wins' applies across all policy types: user, group, role, resource-based
// - Root account is NOT subject to IAM policy denies (but IS subject to SCPs)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Security audit finds over-provisioned IAM permissions.
// Developers were given broad access during initial project setup.
// CISO wants: identify unused permissions, remove them, and prevent future over-provisioning.
//
// Question: What AWS-native tools help identify unused IAM permissions?
// What specific information does each tool provide?
// How would you use them together for a comprehensive audit?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// TOOL 1: IAM Credential Report
//   - Account-level CSV report showing ALL IAM users
//   - Shows: password last used, access key last rotated, access key last used, MFA enabled
//   - Use to identify: users who never logged in, keys unused for 90+ days
//   - Generate via IAM console or CLI: aws iam generate-credential-report
//   - Useful for: identifying inactive accounts, unrotated keys, missing MFA
//
// TOOL 2: IAM Access Advisor (per-user/role service last accessed)
//   - Shows which AWS services a user/role has permissions to access
//   - Shows the LAST DATE each service was actually accessed (from CloudTrail)
//   - Data covers the last 400 days
//   - If a service shows 'Never' or >90 days ago → consider removing that permission
//   - Available in IAM console under each user/role → 'Access Advisor' tab
//
// TOOL 3: IAM Access Analyzer
//   - Analyzes resource-based policies to find resources shared with external entities
//   - Access Analyzer for unused access: identifies unused IAM roles, access keys,
//     passwords, and overly permissive policies (requires enabling in each region)
//   - Generates 'findings' for each over-permission issue with remediation guidance
//   - Can set up EventBridge rules to alert on new findings
//
// COMBINED AUDIT WORKFLOW:
// 1. Generate Credential Report → identify inactive users/keys
// 2. Review Access Advisor for each active user/role → identify unused services
// 3. Enable Access Analyzer → get continuous findings for external access + unused access
// 4. Use IAM Policy Simulator → test what a user can actually do before making changes
// 5. Apply IAM policy changes → remove unused service permissions
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Access Advisor data has up to 4 hours delay and covers last 400 days
// - Credential Report is a snapshot (regenerate for fresh data)
// - Access Analyzer findings include: external access AND unused access findings
// - IAM Policy Simulator: test what policies allow/deny WITHOUT making real API calls

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Multi-account AWS Organization with 15 accounts.
// You want SCPs to:
// - Block resource creation outside us-east-1 and eu-west-1
// - Prevent anyone from disabling CloudTrail
// - Ensure EC2 instances can only be t2.micro or t3.micro
//
// Question: What CAN SCPs do? What CAN'T they do?
// Do SCPs grant permissions? Can they replace IAM policies?
// Does the management account follow SCPs?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// WHAT SCPs CAN DO:
// - Define the MAXIMUM available permissions for all principals in member accounts
// - Apply to all IAM users and roles (including account root user) in member accounts
// - Explicitly DENY specific services, actions, or resources across the entire account
// - Allow only specific regions (blocklist unapproved regions via Deny)
// - Prevent disabling of CloudTrail, GuardDuty, Config (governance guardrails)
// - Restrict EC2 instance types using conditions (StringNotEquals on ec2:InstanceType)
// - Apply to entire OUs or individual accounts within the organization
//
// WHAT SCPs CANNOT DO:
// - GRANT permissions (SCPs never grant; only restrict)
// - Replace IAM policies — users still need identity-based policies to DO anything
// - Override service-linked roles (AWS-managed service roles may bypass SCPs)
// - Apply to the MANAGEMENT (master) account — SCPs have no effect there
// - Affect AWS services themselves (e.g., CloudTrail service operations are excluded)
// - Control resource-based policy cross-account access outside the organization
//
// IMPORTANT EXCEPTIONS:
// - Management account: NOT subject to SCPs (critical security implication)
// - AWS service accounts: Service-linked roles may bypass some SCPs
//
// SCP EXAMPLE — Block regions outside approved list:
// Deny: "*" where aws:RequestedRegion is NOT in ["us-east-1", "eu-west-1"]
// BUT: Allow list some global services (IAM, STS, CloudFront) which need us-east-1
//
// DEFAULT BEHAVIOR:
// - New accounts/OUs inherit FullAWSAccess SCP by default (allows everything)
// - Must explicitly attach restrictive SCPs to limit actions
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - SCPs + IAM policies are both required: SCP sets ceiling, IAM grants actual access
// - Management account is NEVER affected by SCPs — protect it with MFA and limited use
// - FullAWSAccess SCP is the default — it allows all, but still requires IAM policy
// - SCPs support both Allow lists (whitelist) and Deny lists (blacklist)
// - Deny list SCPs are easier to maintain (deny specific risky actions)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// E-commerce site: 100 req/sec normal, 2000 req/sec on Black Friday (2 weeks/year).
// Goal: minimize costs while handling peak traffic automatically.
//
// Question: Which EC2 pricing model(s) should be used for baseline vs spike traffic?
// What Auto Scaling policy type best handles predictable traffic patterns?
// What Auto Scaling policy type handles unpredictable spikes?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// RECOMMENDED ARCHITECTURE: Layered pricing model
//
// BASELINE CAPACITY (year-round, predictable):
//   → Reserved Instances (1-year or 3-year) or Savings Plans
//   → Cover the minimum required instances (say, 10 instances for 100 req/sec)
//   → Savings: up to 72% vs On-Demand (Standard Reserved)
//   → Convertible Reserved: more flexibility to change instance type/OS (less savings)
//
// BLACK FRIDAY SPIKE (predictable 2-week window):
//   → Scheduled Scaling: Pre-scale out BEFORE Black Friday starts
//   → Add On-Demand instances for predictable peak period
//   → Alternatively: Spot Instances for non-critical backend jobs (NOT checkout)
//   → Avoid Spot for checkout service (risk of interruption during payment)
//
// UNEXPECTED SPIKES (dynamic):
//   → Target Tracking Scaling: maintain CPU at 60% or ALB RequestsPerTarget at 1000
//   → Step Scaling: add more aggressive capacity at CPU >80%
//   → Scale-out cooldown: 300 seconds (default); reduce for spiky workloads
//
// AUTO SCALING POLICY TYPES:
// - Scheduled Scaling: scale at specific times (cron) — best for predictable patterns
// - Target Tracking: maintain a metric at a target value — simplest to configure
// - Step Scaling: add/remove based on metric thresholds with different step sizes
// - Predictive Scaling: uses ML to forecast and pre-scale (newer feature)
//
// COST-OPTIMIZED STRATEGY:
// - Reserved/Savings Plan: 10 instances (baseline) → covers year-round steady load
// - On-Demand: scale up to 50 instances for Black Friday week → pay as you go
// - Spot (with Spot Fleet): batch processing, image resizing, non-critical workloads
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Standard Reserved Instances have the best discount but least flexibility
// - Savings Plans (Compute) apply to EC2, Lambda, Fargate automatically — more flexible
// - Use Scheduled Scaling for known peaks; Target Tracking for steady-state optimization
// - Never use Spot for stateful or transactional workloads (payment, sessions)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// EC2 instance with very high I/O performance stores cached data to disk.
// After stop + restart: all cached data is GONE.
// Developer expected the data to persist like a normal hard drive.
//
// Question: What type of storage caused this data loss?
// What is the difference between Instance Store and EBS?
// What is the correct storage type for data that must survive instance stop/restart?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// ROOT CAUSE: Instance Store (ephemeral storage) was used instead of EBS
//
// INSTANCE STORE (ephemeral):
//   - Physically attached to the host server running the EC2 instance
//   - Provides very high performance: millions of IOPS, very low latency
//   - DATA IS LOST when:
//     * Instance is STOPPED (instance may move to different host)
//     * Instance is TERMINATED
//     * Host hardware fails
//   - DATA SURVIVES when:
//     * Instance is REBOOTED (stays on same host)
//   - Cannot be detached and reattached to another instance
//   - Available on storage-optimized families: i3, i4i, d3, h1 instance types
//   - No additional cost (included in instance price)
//
// AMAZON EBS (Elastic Block Store):
//   - Network-attached storage — NOT physically tied to the host
//   - DATA PERSISTS through stop, start, and reboot
//   - Persists independently of the EC2 instance lifecycle
//   - Can be detached from one instance and attached to another
//   - Snapshotted to S3 for backup
//   - Additional cost per GB-month + IOPS
//   - Types: gp3 (general), io2 (high performance), st1 (throughput), sc1 (cold)
//
// SOLUTION FOR THE SCENARIO:
//   - Use EBS io2 Block Express for high IOPS with data persistence
//   - io2 supports up to 256,000 IOPS per volume
//   - For truly ephemeral cache: Instance Store is fine and cheaper
//   - For data that must survive instance lifecycle: always use EBS
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Instance Store: highest performance, ephemeral (lost on stop/terminate)
// - EBS: persistent, network-attached, survives stop/terminate
// - Stop ≠ Reboot: stop can move instance to new host (Instance Store data lost)
// - Reboot stays on same host (Instance Store survives reboot but not stop)
// - AMI backed by Instance Store cannot be stopped, only terminated

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// EC2 instances in private subnet (no public IP, no direct internet route).
// They need OUTBOUND internet access to:
// - Download OS updates (yum/apt)
// - Pull Docker images from Docker Hub
// - Call third-party REST APIs
// But must NOT be directly reachable from the internet.
//
// Question: What AWS service enables this? How does it work?
// Where does this service need to be placed in the VPC architecture?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: NAT Gateway in a Public Subnet
//
// ARCHITECTURE:
// Internet → Internet Gateway (IGW)
//                ↑
//          [Public Subnet]
//          NAT Gateway (with Elastic IP)
//                ↑
//          [Private Subnet]
//          EC2 instances → Route: 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway
//
// SETUP STEPS:
// 1. Create a NAT Gateway in a PUBLIC subnet
//    - Public subnet must have route to Internet Gateway (0.0.0.0/0 → IGW)
//    - Assign an Elastic IP to the NAT Gateway
// 2. Update Private Subnet Route Table:
//    - Add route: 0.0.0.0/0 → nat-gateway-id
// 3. EC2 instances in private subnet now have OUTBOUND internet access
//    - Inbound connections from internet are NOT possible (NAT is one-directional)
//
// HOW NAT WORKS:
// - EC2 sends packet to internet (source: private IP 10.0.1.5)
// - NAT Gateway translates source IP to its Elastic IP
// - Response comes back to Elastic IP → NAT Gateway translates back to 10.0.1.5
// - Internet never sees or can reach the private IP directly
//
// NAT GATEWAY vs NAT INSTANCE:
// - NAT Gateway: AWS-managed, highly available, scales automatically, no patching
// - NAT Instance: self-managed EC2, must patch, configure, handle HA yourself
// - For exam: NAT Gateway is ALWAYS preferred (managed service)
//
// HA CONSIDERATION:
// - Create one NAT Gateway PER Availability Zone
// - If single AZ NAT Gateway fails, private instances in other AZs lose internet
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - NAT Gateway must be in PUBLIC subnet, route traffic FROM private subnet TO NAT GW
// - NAT Gateway is for OUTBOUND only — inbound from internet not possible
// - Elastic IP required for NAT Gateway (static public IP)
// - NAT Gateway is NOT free — charged per hour + data processing fee
// - Security Groups do NOT apply to NAT Gateway; use NACLs for subnet-level control

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Four workloads requiring different EC2 pricing models:
// 1. Production web server: 24/7, must not be interrupted, 1+ year commitment OK
// 2. Dev environment: M-F, 8 hours/day, can be stopped at night/weekends
// 3. Batch data processing: can run anytime, can be paused/retried if interrupted
// 4. Unpredictable spike workload: variable load, no commitments, pay-as-you-go
//
// Question: Which EC2 pricing model is MOST cost-effective for each?
// Explain the tradeoffs of each model.
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// 1. PRODUCTION WEB SERVER (24/7, no interruption, 1-year commitment):
//    → Reserved Instances (Standard 1-year) or Compute Savings Plans
//    → Savings: 40-72% vs On-Demand
//    → Standard RI: locked to instance type/region; highest discount
//    → Convertible RI: can change instance type/OS; ~54% discount
//    → Compute Savings Plan: applies to EC2, Lambda, Fargate; ~66% discount
//    → Best for: predictable, continuous workloads
//
// 2. DEV ENVIRONMENT (weekdays only, 8hrs/day = ~40hrs/week = ~24% of month):
//    → On-Demand Instances (or schedule stop/start)
//    → With scheduling: only pay for ~40 hours/week (vs 168 hrs/week for Reserved)
//    → Reserved would be WASTEFUL: paying for 168 hours but using only 40
//    → Savings vs Reserved: pay only for actual hours used
//    → Best for: workloads with significant idle time
//
// 3. BATCH DATA PROCESSING (fault-tolerant, interruptible):
//    → Spot Instances
//    → Savings: up to 90% vs On-Demand
//    → Risk: 2-minute interruption notice when AWS needs the capacity back
//    → Must handle interruptions: checkpointing, restarting from last checkpoint
//    → Use Spot Fleet or EC2 Fleet for multiple instance types/AZs (higher availability)
//    → Best for: stateless, fault-tolerant, flexible start-time workloads
//
// 4. UNPREDICTABLE SPIKES (variable, no commitment):
//    → On-Demand Instances
//    → No upfront commitment, pay by the second (minimum 60 seconds)
//    → Most expensive per hour, but no waste during idle periods
//    → Best for: unpredictable workloads, short-term experiments, new applications
//
// PRICING MODEL SUMMARY:
// On-Demand  → flexibility, highest cost per hour
// Reserved   → commitment (1 or 3 years), lowest cost for steady-state
// Savings Plan → like Reserved but more flexible (applies automatically)
// Spot       → up to 90% discount, but interruptible
// Dedicated Host → compliance/licensing requirements, most expensive
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Running 24/7 for 1+ year → Reserved/Savings Plan always cheaper than On-Demand
// - Spot = cheapest but interruptible; never use for databases or interactive apps
// - Dev environments that stop: On-Demand is better than Reserved
// - Spot interruption: 2-minute warning via metadata + EventBridge notification

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// You need to:
// 1. Run a shell script automatically when an EC2 instance first launches
//    to install software and configure the application
// 2. Have a running application query its own instance ID, region,
//    and IAM role credentials at runtime without hardcoding values
//
// Question: Which EC2 feature handles bootstrapping (#1)?
// Which handles runtime self-discovery (#2)?
// How does each work and how do you access each?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// EC2 USER DATA (bootstrapping):
//   - Shell scripts or cloud-init directives that run at FIRST LAUNCH
//   - Passed to the instance at launch time (cannot be easily changed after launch)
//   - Runs as ROOT user by default
//   - Use for: installing packages, configuring services, pulling code from S3/Git
//   - Access via IMDS: http://169.254.169.254/latest/user-data
//   - Re-run on every boot: add cloud-init directive or configure in script
//   - Max size: 16 KB (for bootstrapping scripts)
//   - Not encrypted by default — avoid putting secrets in User Data
//
// INSTANCE METADATA SERVICE (IMDS):
//   - HTTP endpoint at 169.254.169.254 (link-local, only accessible from the instance)
//   - Provides: instance ID, AZ, region, public/private IP, IAM credentials, etc.
//   - Query at runtime: curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id
//   - Common paths:
//     /latest/meta-data/instance-id         → i-0abc123def456789
//     /latest/meta-data/placement/region    → us-east-1
//     /latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ROLE_NAME → temp credentials
//     /latest/meta-data/local-ipv4          → private IP
//     /latest/meta-data/public-ipv4         → public IP
//
// IMDSv2 (Recommended, more secure):
//   - IMDSv1: simple HTTP GET requests (vulnerable to SSRF attacks)
//   - IMDSv2: requires first getting a session token via PUT request
//     Step 1: TOKEN=$(curl -X PUT -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600"
//                  http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token)
//     Step 2: curl -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN"
//                  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id
//   - Force IMDSv2 only by setting HttpTokens=required in instance settings
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - User Data: runs at launch, used for bootstrapping, plain text
// - IMDS: runtime HTTP service, 169.254.169.254, never expose to public internet
// - IAM role credentials are fetched from IMDS (auto-refreshed, no code changes)
// - IMDSv2 prevents SSRF attacks by requiring session tokens (PUT before GET)
// - User Data is limited to 16KB; for large scripts, store in S3 and download in User Data

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Multi-tier app: public web servers in public subnet, RDS in private subnet.
// NACL configured with: Allow inbound TCP 443, but users still cannot connect.
// Security Group allows all traffic.
//
// Question: Why are users still blocked despite the NACL inbound rule?
// What is the key difference between Security Groups and NACLs?
// How do you properly configure NACLs for HTTPS traffic?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// ROOT CAUSE: NACL is STATELESS — return traffic needs an explicit outbound rule
//
// STATEFUL vs STATELESS:
// Security Groups (STATEFUL):
//   - Track connection state — if inbound traffic is allowed, the RESPONSE is
//     automatically allowed outbound (no explicit outbound rule needed)
//   - Apply at the INSTANCE level (ENI)
//   - Rules: Allow only (no explicit Deny; unmatched = denied)
//   - Evaluated as a whole — all rules evaluated before decision
//
// Network ACLs (STATELESS):
//   - No connection tracking — each packet evaluated independently
//   - INBOUND rules apply to incoming packets; OUTBOUND rules apply to outgoing
//   - If you allow inbound port 443, you must ALSO allow outbound for response traffic
//   - Response uses EPHEMERAL PORTS (1024-65535) not port 443
//   - Apply at the SUBNET level
//   - Rules: Allow OR Deny; evaluated in order by rule number (lowest first)
//   - First matching rule wins; end with explicit deny all
//
// FIX FOR THE SCENARIO:
// Current config: Allow inbound TCP 443 → HTTPS request arrives ✓
// Missing config: Allow outbound TCP 1024-65535 (ephemeral ports) → response can leave ✓
//
// PROPER NACL CONFIG FOR HTTPS WEB SERVER:
// Inbound Rules:
//   Rule 100: Allow TCP 443 from 0.0.0.0/0 (inbound HTTPS requests)
//   Rule 110: Allow TCP 1024-65535 from 0.0.0.0/0 (response from remote servers)
// Outbound Rules:
//   Rule 100: Allow TCP 443 to 0.0.0.0/0 (if server initiates HTTPS calls)
//   Rule 110: Allow TCP 1024-65535 to 0.0.0.0/0 (response to clients requesting 443)
//   Rule *: Deny all (default rule, always present)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Security Groups: stateful, instance-level, allow only, all rules evaluated
// - NACLs: stateless, subnet-level, allow+deny, rules processed in order
// - NACLs always need BOTH inbound AND outbound rules for bidirectional traffic
// - Ephemeral port range: 1024-65535 (Windows) or 1024-65535 (Linux)
// - Default NACL: allows all inbound and outbound (newly created VPC)
// - Custom NACL: denies all inbound and outbound by default (newly created NACL)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// E-commerce app on EC2 behind ALB.
// Current: in-place deployment causes 10-15 minutes downtime.
// Goal: zero-downtime blue/green deployments with instant rollback capability.
//
// Question: Which AWS service orchestrates blue/green deployments on EC2?
// How does traffic shifting work in a blue/green deployment?
// How does instant rollback work?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SERVICE: AWS CodeDeploy with Blue/Green Deployment on EC2
//
// HOW BLUE/GREEN WORKS ON EC2:
// BLUE environment: current production instances (Auto Scaling Group)
// GREEN environment: new Auto Scaling Group with updated code provisioned by CodeDeploy
//
// DEPLOYMENT FLOW:
// 1. CodeDeploy creates a NEW Auto Scaling Group (green) with same capacity as blue
// 2. New instances in green group are provisioned and the new code is deployed
// 3. New instances are registered with the ALB target group
// 4. Health checks run on green instances
// 5. Traffic is gradually shifted from blue (old) to green (new) ALB target group
// 6. Traffic shifting options:
//    - AllAtOnce: 100% immediately to green
//    - HalfAtATime: 50% at a time
//    - OneAtATime: gradual shifting
// 7. Blue instances remain running during the configurable 'wait time'
//    (default 1 hour — can be overridden for immediate termination or extended)
// 8. If validation passes: blue instances terminated
//    If issues found: traffic shifted BACK to blue instantly (rollback)
//
// INSTANT ROLLBACK:
// - Blue instances are NOT terminated until wait period expires
// - Rollback = one ALB target group switch, takes seconds
// - No need to redeploy code or provision new instances
//
// ALTERNATIVES:
// - Elastic Beanstalk: managed platform with built-in blue/green via environment swap (URL swap)
// - Route 53 weighted routing: shift DNS traffic between two environments
// - ALB weighted target groups: split traffic at L7 level
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - CodeDeploy Blue/Green on EC2 uses TWO Auto Scaling Groups
// - ALB handles traffic splitting between blue and green target groups
// - Blue instances stay alive during wait period (instant rollback possible)
// - appspec.yml defines hooks (BeforeInstall, AfterInstall, ValidateService etc.)
// - CodeDeploy is free for EC2/on-prem; CodeDeploy for Lambda/ECS costs extra

starterCode:
// Three workloads needing EC2 Placement Groups:
// 1. HPC: parallel computation, needs lowest possible network latency between all nodes
// 2. Critical DB: 7 nodes, need MAXIMUM hardware failure isolation (different physical racks)
// 3. Big data: 48 instances, need rack-level isolation, can tolerate some correlated failures
//    within a rack, but different groups should be on different racks
//
// Question: Which Placement Group type (Cluster/Spread/Partition) suits each workload?
// What are the limitations of each type?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// WORKLOAD 1 (HPC, lowest latency): → CLUSTER Placement Group
//   - All instances placed in same Availability Zone, same rack (ideally same host)
//   - Very low latency (10 Gbps network between instances vs 5 Gbps standard)
//   - Enhanced networking (ENA/SR-IOV) recommended
//   - LIMITATION: If the rack fails, ALL instances fail simultaneously
//   - LIMITATION: Instances must be launched together (capacity not guaranteed if added later)
//   - LIMITATION: Not all instance types supported
//   - Best for: HPC, distributed GPU training, tightly-coupled parallel jobs
//
// WORKLOAD 2 (Critical DB, 7 nodes, max isolation): → SPREAD Placement Group
//   - Each instance on a DIFFERENT physical rack with separate power/network
//   - Maximum fault isolation — one rack failure affects only one instance
//   - LIMITATION: Maximum 7 instances PER AZ per Spread Placement Group
//   - Can span multiple AZs (up to 7 per AZ = 21 total across 3 AZs)
//   - Best for: small critical clusters, primary/replica DBs, critical microservices
//
// WORKLOAD 3 (Big data, 48 instances, rack-level isolation at scale): → PARTITION
//   - Instances divided into logical PARTITIONS (up to 7 per AZ)
//   - Each partition is on different physical racks (within the AZ)
//   - Instances within same partition may share a rack (correlated failure within partition)
//   - Instances in DIFFERENT partitions have rack-level isolation from each other
//   - LIMITATION: Up to 7 partitions per AZ
//   - No limit per partition on instance count (scales to hundreds)
//   - Applications can discover partition topology via IMDS metadata
//   - Best for: HDFS, Cassandra, Kafka — large distributed systems with replication
//
// SUMMARY TABLE:
// Cluster:   Same rack, max performance, poor fault isolation, max instances = no hard limit
// Spread:    Different racks, max isolation, 7 instances/AZ LIMIT
// Partition: Groups of instances per rack, rack isolation between groups, scales to 100s
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Spread max: 7 instances per AZ (critical limit for exam questions)
// - Cluster max latency: 10 Gbps enhanced networking within the group
// - Partition: instances can query their partition number via IMDS (for data placement)
// - Placement groups are free; no additional charge

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// S3 log bucket with access patterns:
// - 0-7 days: frequently accessed (debugging)
// - 7-30 days: occasionally accessed
// - 30-90 days: rarely accessed, retrieval within hours OK
// - 90-365 days: almost never accessed, retrieval within 12 hours OK
// - 365+ days: delete automatically
//
// Question: Which S3 storage class suits each tier?
// How do you automate the transitions?
// What does each storage class cost/tradeoff?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// STORAGE CLASS SELECTION:
// 0-7 days:    S3 Standard
//   → Default, millisecond access, 99.99% availability, highest cost
//   → No retrieval fee, appropriate for frequent access
//
// 7-30 days:   S3 Standard-IA (Infrequent Access)
//   → Same performance (millisecond retrieval) as Standard
//   → Lower storage cost, but has per-GB RETRIEVAL fee
//   → Minimum storage duration: 30 days (charged 30 days minimum)
//
// 30-90 days:  S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval
//   → Similar to Standard-IA pricing but lower storage cost
//   → Millisecond retrieval (unlike other Glacier classes)
//   → Minimum storage duration: 90 days
//   → Per-GB retrieval fee applies
//
// 90-365 days: S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval
//   → Very low storage cost (~$0.004/GB/month)
//   → Retrieval options: Expedited (1-5 min), Standard (3-5 hr), Bulk (5-12 hr)
//   → Minimum storage duration: 90 days
//   → Higher retrieval fee than IA classes
//
// 365+ days:   Delete via Lifecycle Expiration rule
//
// LIFECYCLE POLICY CONFIGURATION:
// Rule 1: Transition to Standard-IA after 7 days
// Rule 2: Transition to Glacier Instant Retrieval after 30 days
// Rule 3: Transition to Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days
// Rule 4: Expire (delete) objects after 365 days
//
// IMPORTANT LIFECYCLE NOTES:
// - Minimum days before transition: 30 days from Standard to Standard-IA/One Zone-IA
//   (cannot transition before 30 days — lifecycle enforces this)
// - Glacier Flexible Retrieval: objects must be restored before access (not inline)
// - Restore creates a temporary copy in Standard for the restore duration
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - S3 Intelligent-Tiering: auto-moves objects between tiers based on actual access
//   (good when access pattern is unknown, small per-object monitoring fee)
// - Standard-IA/One Zone-IA: retrieval fee applies — avoid for frequent access
// - Glacier classes: not suitable for real-time access without restore operation
// - One Zone-IA: cheaper than Standard-IA but single AZ (99.5% availability)
// - Minimum storage duration charges: Standard-IA = 30 days, Glacier = 90 days

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Photo app: users upload images via browser.
// Current: browser → App Server (EC2) → S3 (wastes server bandwidth)
// Goal: browser uploads DIRECTLY to S3 without exposing credentials
//       and with controlled access (only to their own folder, limited time)
//
// Question: What AWS mechanism enables secure direct-to-S3 uploads from browsers?
// How is it generated? What information does it contain?
// How do you limit what can be uploaded (size, type, location)?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: S3 Presigned URLs (for GET/PUT) or Presigned POST (for browser forms)
//
// PRESIGNED URL CONCEPT:
// - Server-side code generates a time-limited URL that includes:
//   * Bucket and object key (where to upload)
//   * Expiry timestamp (e.g., valid for 15 minutes)
//   * Signature derived from the server's AWS credentials
// - Client uses this URL to PUT directly to S3
// - URL is valid only for the specified duration and specific object key
// - No AWS credentials exposed to browser — the signature is embedded in the URL
//
// PRESIGNED PUT URL FLOW:
// 1. User requests upload to app server
// 2. App server generates presigned URL for s3://bucket/user-123/photo.jpg
//    with 15-minute expiry
// 3. App server returns presigned URL to browser
// 4. Browser PUTs image directly to S3 using presigned URL
// 5. Upload goes directly browser → S3 (bypasses app server)
//
// PRESIGNED POST (for HTML form uploads):
// - Server generates a Presigned POST policy document:
//   * Allowed bucket and key prefix
//   * Maximum file size
//   * Allowed content types
//   * Expiry time
// - Browser submits HTML form directly to S3 with the policy + signature
// - S3 validates the policy before accepting the upload
// - Allows conditional checks (content-type must be image/jpeg, max-size 5MB)
//
// SECURITY CONTROLS:
// - Key prefix: force upload to user-specific path (user-123/)
// - Expiry: short TTL (15-60 minutes) limits window of use
// - Content-Length-Range condition: enforce max file size
// - Content-Type condition: only allow image/* types
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Presigned URLs are generated using the SIGNER's credentials
//   (if IAM role generates it, the role must have s3:PutObject)
// - If the IAM role's credentials expire before the presigned URL, the URL fails
// - Max expiry: 7 days for IAM User signatures; ~6 hours for IAM Role temp credentials
// - Presigned URL inherits the signer's permissions — no additional IAM setup for bucket
// - S3 bucket policy does NOT need to be public; presigned URL bypasses bucket policy

starterCode:
// Two replication needs:
// 1. Replicate from us-east-1 to eu-west-1 (disaster recovery, compliance)
// 2. Replicate from prod-bucket (us-east-1) to analytics-bucket (us-east-1)
//
// Question: What are the two types of S3 replication?
// What is the mandatory prerequisite for BOTH types?
// What objects are replicated vs not replicated?
// Is replication retroactive (does it copy existing objects)?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// TWO REPLICATION TYPES:
// CRR (Cross-Region Replication):
//   - Source and destination buckets are in DIFFERENT AWS regions
//   - Use cases: disaster recovery, latency reduction, compliance/data sovereignty
//   - Workload 1 solution: CRR from us-east-1 to eu-west-1
//
// SRR (Same-Region Replication):
//   - Source and destination buckets are in the SAME AWS region (even same/different account)
//   - Use cases: log aggregation, live replication between prod and test, analytics copy
//   - Workload 2 solution: SRR from prod-bucket to analytics-bucket in us-east-1
//
// MANDATORY PREREQUISITE:
// - VERSIONING must be ENABLED on BOTH source and destination buckets
// - No exceptions — replication will NOT work without versioning
// - Each replicated object retains its version ID at the destination
//
// WHAT IS REPLICATED:
// - All new objects created AFTER replication rule is enabled
// - Objects with all metadata, ACLs (optionally), tags, encryption
//
// WHAT IS NOT REPLICATED BY DEFAULT:
// - Objects already in the bucket BEFORE rule was created (not retroactive)
// - Objects in Glacier / Glacier Deep Archive storage class
// - Objects that are already replicas (no re-replication of replicated objects)
// - Objects without the replication permission on the source bucket owner
//
// FOR EXISTING OBJECTS:
// - Use S3 Batch Replication (one-time operation to replicate pre-existing objects)
// - Or use S3 sync command (CLI)
//
// REPLICATION TIME CONTROL (RTC):
// - Optional add-on that guarantees 99.99% of objects replicated within 15 minutes
// - Additional cost; standard replication has no SLA time guarantee
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Versioning required on BOTH buckets — this is the #1 exam trap
// - CRR = different regions, SRR = same region
// - Replication is NOT retroactive (use S3 Batch Replication for existing objects)
// - Delete markers can be replicated (optional, disabled by default)
// - Source bucket must have an IAM role allowing s3:ReplicateObject on destination

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// S3 bucket contains sensitive financial data.
// Requirement: ONLY accessible from the company's specific VPC (vpc-12345)
// - Public internet access: BLOCKED (even for valid IAM users accessing from home)
// - VPC application servers: ALLOWED (seamless, private network access)
// - Data must not traverse the public internet even from within AWS
//
// Question: What two AWS components work together to achieve this?
// What is the bucket policy condition that enforces VPC-only access?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: VPC Gateway Endpoint for S3 + S3 Bucket Policy with VPC condition
//
// COMPONENT 1: VPC Gateway Endpoint for S3
//   - Creates a private route inside your VPC to S3
//   - Traffic stays within AWS network — never traverses public internet
//   - No data transfer charges (unlike internet egress)
//   - Add endpoint route to VPC route table: destination s3.amazonaws.com → endpoint
//   - Cost: FREE (Gateway Endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB have no hourly charge)
//
// COMPONENT 2: S3 Bucket Policy with VPC Condition
// {
//   "Effect": "Deny",
//   "Principal": "*",
//   "Action": "s3:*",
//   "Resource": [
//     "arn:aws:s3:::financial-data-bucket",
//     "arn:aws:s3:::financial-data-bucket/*"
//   ],
//   "Condition": {
//     "StringNotEquals": {
//       "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-12345abc"
//     }
//   }
// }
//
// OR use VPC ID instead of VPC Endpoint ID:
//   "aws:SourceVpc": "vpc-12345"
//
// HOW IT WORKS:
// - VPC application servers access S3 via the Gateway Endpoint (private route)
// - Requests include the VPC Endpoint ID automatically
// - Bucket policy allows: requests from vpce-12345abc
// - Bucket policy DENIES: all other requests (internet, other VPCs, other endpoints)
// - Even valid IAM users accessing from outside the VPC will be denied
//
// GATEWAY vs INTERFACE ENDPOINT for S3:
// - Gateway Endpoint: free, modifies VPC route table, works only within the same region
// - Interface Endpoint (PrivateLink): charged per hour + data, works cross-region,
//   supports on-premises access via Direct Connect/VPN, DNS-based routing
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - VPC Gateway Endpoint for S3 is FREE — preferred for same-region S3 access
// - Use aws:SourceVpce (endpoint ID) or aws:SourceVpc (VPC ID) in bucket policy
// - Both bucket AND endpoint policies can restrict access (defense in depth)
// - VPC Endpoint policy: what services/buckets the endpoint can access
// - Bucket policy: which VPC/endpoint can access the bucket
// - No NAT Gateway needed for S3 access via Gateway Endpoint (saves cost)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// S3 static website hosting enabled.
// URL returns 403 Forbidden.
// Developer checks: bucket policy allows s3:GetObject for Principal: *
// But still getting 403.
//
// Question: What are ALL the possible causes of 403 errors on S3 static websites?
// In what order should you check them?
// What is the 'Block Public Access' setting and how does it interact with bucket policies?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SYSTEMATIC DIAGNOSIS OF S3 STATIC WEBSITE 403 ERRORS:
//
// CHECK 1: S3 Block Public Access Settings (MOST COMMON CAUSE)
//   - S3 has FOUR Block Public Access settings at ACCOUNT level AND bucket level:
//     * BlockPublicAcls: blocks new public ACLs
//     * IgnorePublicAcls: ignores existing public ACLs
//     * BlockPublicPolicy: blocks new public bucket policies
//     * RestrictPublicBuckets: restricts access even if bucket policy allows public
//   - If ANY of these is ON, the bucket policy allowing public access is OVERRIDDEN
//   - FIX: Disable relevant Block Public Access settings at bucket level
//   - Account-level Block Public Access overrides ALL bucket-level settings
//
// CHECK 2: Bucket Policy Missing or Incorrect
//   - Required policy for static website:
//     Effect: Allow, Principal: "*", Action: s3:GetObject,
//     Resource: arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*
//   - Common mistake: Resource is arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name (missing /*)
//     → allows listing bucket but not GetObject on individual files
//   - Policy must cover objects (/* suffix), not just the bucket
//
// CHECK 3: Object-Level ACL Issues
//   - If IgnorePublicAcls is OFF but BlockPublicPolicy is also OFF,
//     object ACLs must also allow public read
//   - Modern best practice: use bucket policies, not ACLs (ACLs are legacy)
//
// CHECK 4: Object Does Not Exist (404-but-looks-like-403)
//   - S3 static website returns 403 (not 404) for missing files
//     when bucket is NOT public (security: doesn't reveal object existence)
//   - When bucket IS public: missing files return 404
//   - Check the exact key path is correct
//
// CHECK 5: CORS Configuration (for browser requests)
//   - If 403 appears in browser DevTools for cross-origin requests,
//     it may actually be a CORS issue masquerading as 403
//   - Add CORS configuration to the S3 bucket allowing your domain's origin
//
// CHECK 6: CloudFront or Custom Domain Issues
//   - If using CloudFront in front of S3, verify OAC/OAI is configured correctly
//   - CloudFront Origin Access Control requires bucket policy to allow CF service
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Block Public Access is the #1 cause of 403 on new S3 buckets (enabled by default)
// - Account-level BPA overrides bucket-level settings
// - s3:GetObject permission on /* (objects) is required, not just on bucket ARN
// - Static website hosting and REST API endpoint are DIFFERENT URLs
// - Static website: http://bucket.s3-website.region.amazonaws.com (no HTTPS)
// - REST endpoint: https://bucket.s3.region.amazonaws.com (HTTPS, different behavior)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// S3 bucket receives new photo uploads.
// Automatically trigger:
// 1. Thumbnail generation (immediate processing)
// 2. Email notification to admin team (fan-out)
// 3. Queue-based processing for other downstream services
//
// Question: What are the S3 Event Notification destination options?
// Which target is best for each use case?
// How do you fan-out to multiple destinations from a single S3 event?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// S3 EVENT NOTIFICATION DIRECT TARGETS:
// 1. AWS Lambda Function
//    - Synchronous invocation from S3
//    - Best for: immediate processing (thumbnail generation)
//    - S3 passes event JSON with bucket name, object key, size, event type
//    - Lambda processes the event (read source, generate thumbnail, write to output bucket)
//
// 2. Amazon SQS Queue
//    - S3 puts event message into SQS queue
//    - Best for: decoupled, queue-based downstream processing
//    - Downstream services poll SQS at their own pace
//    - Cannot use FIFO queues for S3 notifications (Standard queues only)
//
// 3. Amazon SNS Topic
//    - S3 publishes event to SNS topic
//    - Best for: fan-out to multiple subscribers
//    - SNS subscribers: SQS, Lambda, Email, HTTP endpoints
//    - Enables one S3 event to reach multiple destinations
//
// 4. Amazon EventBridge (more advanced routing)
//    - All S3 events can be forwarded to EventBridge
//    - Supports: more complex filtering, multiple rules, archive/replay
//    - Routes to many more targets (Step Functions, Kinesis, etc.)
//    - Recommended for complex event routing requirements
//
// ARCHITECTURE FOR THE SCENARIO:
// S3 PUT event → SNS Topic
//                    ├── Lambda (thumbnail generation — immediate)
//                    ├── SQS (downstream service processing queue)
//                    └── Email subscription (admin notifications)
//
// OR using EventBridge:
// S3 PUT event → EventBridge
//                    ├── Rule 1: prefix=photos/ → Lambda (thumbnails)
//                    ├── Rule 2: prefix=photos/ → SQS (processing)
//                    └── Rule 3: prefix=photos/ → SNS (notifications)
//
// CONFIGURATION REQUIREMENTS:
// - Resource policy on Lambda/SQS/SNS must allow S3 service to invoke/publish
// - S3 notifications can filter by: prefix (folder path) and suffix (file extension)
// - Enable S3 EventBridge integration in bucket properties for EventBridge routing
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - S3 direct targets: Lambda, SQS (Standard only), SNS
// - SQS FIFO NOT supported for S3 notifications
// - For fan-out: use SNS topic as the S3 target, then SNS fans out to multiple
// - EventBridge provides richer filtering and more destination options
// - S3 event notifications are 'at least once' delivery (may duplicate)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Critical config files in S3 were accidentally deleted (no versioning was enabled).
// Plan: enable versioning + MFA Delete for maximum protection.
//
// Question: What does S3 Versioning protect against?
// What is a delete marker and how do you restore a deleted (versioned) object?
// What does MFA Delete add on top of versioning?
// What are the storage cost implications of versioning?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// S3 VERSIONING:
// - Stores ALL versions of an object (including all writes and deletes)
// - Protects against: accidental deletion, accidental overwrites, application bugs
// - Once enabled, versioning cannot be fully DISABLED, only SUSPENDED
//   (suspending stops new versions but keeps existing ones)
//
// DELETE BEHAVIOR WITH VERSIONING:
// - DELETE without version ID: S3 adds a 'delete marker' (a special zero-byte version)
//   * The object appears deleted to s3:GetObject requests
//   * The actual data versions still exist in S3
//   * Listing without showing versions will not show the object
// - DELETE with specific version ID: permanently removes that specific version
//   * Cannot be undone
//
// RESTORING A 'DELETED' VERSIONED OBJECT:
// Option A: Delete the delete marker
//   - List object versions: GET /?versions to find the delete marker version ID
//   - DELETE the delete marker (by specifying its version ID)
//   - Result: latest 'real' version becomes current again
// Option B: Copy a previous version to the same key
//   - GET the desired version: GET /key?versionId=abc123
//   - PUT it back as the latest version (no version ID needed)
//
// MFA DELETE:
// - An ADDITIONAL protection layer on top of versioning
// - Requires MFA authentication to:
//   1. Permanently delete a specific object version
//   2. Change the versioning state (enable/suspend/disable)
// - Does NOT affect normal operations (upload, download, list)
// - Must be enabled by the BUCKET OWNER (root account) via CLI (not console)
//   (aws s3api put-bucket-versioning with MFADelete=Enabled and --mfa argument)
// - Protects against: compromised credentials being used to delete version history
//
// COST IMPLICATIONS:
// - Each version is stored as a full separate object (no deduplication)
// - Even deleted objects incur storage costs (versions remain until explicitly removed)
// - Use lifecycle rules: expire non-current versions after N days to control costs
//   (e.g., delete non-current versions older than 30 days)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Versioning once enabled: can only be suspended, not fully removed
// - Delete without versionId = delete marker (reversible, data intact)
// - Delete WITH versionId = permanent deletion (irreversible)
// - MFA Delete must be configured by root account; IAM users cannot enable it
// - MFA Delete requires hardware or virtual MFA device (not SMS)

starterCode:
// Three data sensitivity levels with different encryption needs:
// 1. General medical forms: auto-encrypted, AWS manages everything, no key audit needed
// 2. Patient diagnosis records: must audit every access (who decrypted what, when)
// 3. Ultra-sensitive genetic data: company holds encryption keys, never shared with AWS
//
// Question: Which S3 encryption option is best for each scenario?
// Where are keys stored for each option?
// What is the performance and cost implication of each?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// OPTION 1: SSE-S3 (Server-Side Encryption with S3-Managed Keys)
//   → Use for: General medical forms (scenario 1)
//   - AWS S3 manages all key creation, rotation, and encryption
//   - No key management required by customer
//   - AES-256 encryption, unique key per object + master key managed by S3
//   - No CloudTrail audit for encryption operations (S3 API calls logged, not key use)
//   - No additional cost (included in S3)
//   - Header to request: x-amz-server-side-encryption: AES256
//
// OPTION 2: SSE-KMS (Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS Keys)
//   → Use for: Patient diagnosis records requiring audit trail (scenario 2)
//   - Customer controls the KMS key (CMK — Customer Managed Key)
//   - Every encrypt/decrypt generates a CloudTrail event: who used the key, when, from where
//   - Can set key policy: restrict which IAM roles can use the key
//   - Can enable automatic key rotation (annual)
//   - KMS API call limits: 5,500-30,000 requests/sec per region (potential throttling)
//   - Additional cost: ~$0.03 per 10,000 KMS API calls + key storage
//   - Header to request: x-amz-server-side-encryption: aws:kms
//   - Can also use S3-managed KMS key (aws/s3) — no cost for key storage
//
// OPTION 3: SSE-C (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys)
//   → Use for: Ultra-sensitive genetic data (scenario 3)
//   - Customer generates and provides encryption key with EVERY request
//   - AWS uses the key to encrypt/decrypt, then DISCARDS it (never stored by AWS)
//   - Customer must securely store and manage their own keys
//   - AWS console CANNOT view SSE-C encrypted objects (no key access)
//   - Only accessible via HTTPS (HTTP requests with SSE-C are rejected)
//   - No CloudTrail audit for key use (key is transient)
//   - Client must supply: x-amz-server-side-encryption-customer-key header
//
// OPTION 4: Client-Side Encryption
//   - Data is encrypted BY THE CLIENT before uploading to S3
//   - AWS S3 stores the encrypted blob — has no knowledge of the plaintext
//   - Client decrypts after downloading
//   - Maximum isolation: not even S3 service sees plaintext
//   - Customer manages keys and encryption library
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - SSE-S3: simplest, no audit trail for key use, no extra cost
// - SSE-KMS: audit trail via CloudTrail, key policy control, extra cost per API call
// - SSE-C: customer manages keys, HTTPS required, AWS never stores key
// - Default encryption on bucket: can set SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS as bucket default
// - Bucket key: reduces SSE-KMS costs by creating per-bucket data key (fewer KMS calls)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Lambda triggered by S3 uploads, processes 2-8 GB video metadata files.
// Problem: 15-minute Lambda timeout is not enough for the largest files.
// Constraint: Lambda max timeout is 15 minutes (cannot be extended).
//
// Question: What are the options for processing large files that exceed Lambda's timeout?
// What is the S3 byte-range feature and how does it help?
// How does SQS help with async processing of large files?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTIONS FOR LARGE FILE PROCESSING (exceeding 15-min Lambda timeout):
//
// OPTION 1: S3 Byte-Range Reads (parallel Lambda processing)
//   - Divide the large file into N chunks using byte ranges
//   - Invoke N Lambda functions in parallel, each processing one range
//   - Each Lambda reads only its range: GET /object with Range: bytes=0-500000000
//   - Fan-out via Step Functions parallel state or SNS+Lambda
//   - Aggregate results in a final Lambda or DynamoDB
//   - Works for: files where data can be processed in independent chunks
//
// OPTION 2: SQS Queue for Async Decoupling
//   - Lambda triggered by S3: instead of processing, just enqueues the job into SQS
//   - Worker (EC2, ECS, Fargate) polls SQS and does the actual heavy processing
//   - Worker has no timeout limitation (can run for hours)
//   - SQS visibility timeout: set to > expected processing time (max 12 hours)
//   - DLQ: failed jobs moved to DLQ after N retries for manual investigation
//   - Best for: jobs requiring longer than 15 minutes with full retry semantics
//
// OPTION 3: Use AWS Fargate or ECS Tasks (for truly long jobs)
//   - Lambda receives S3 event → triggers a Fargate task via ECS RunTask API
//   - Fargate task processes the file with no timeout restriction
//   - More control over memory (up to 120 GB for Fargate) and CPU
//   - Best for: jobs that need >15 min AND large memory
//
// OPTION 4: AWS Step Functions
//   - Orchestrate multi-step workflows with Lambda functions
//   - Each Step processes a portion (using Map state for parallel processing)
//   - Step Functions workflow can run up to 1 year
//   - Handles retries, error catching, and state tracking
//
// OPTION 5: Increase Lambda Memory (within 15 min constraint)
//   - Lambda memory: 128MB to 10,240MB (10 GB)
//   - More memory = proportionally more vCPU (linear relationship)
//   - Processing speed increases with more CPU cores available to Lambda
//   - May bring 15-min-borderline jobs within the timeout
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Lambda hard timeout = 15 minutes (no way around it)
// - For jobs > 15 min: SQS → Fargate/ECS or Step Functions
// - S3 Byte-Range reads enable parallel processing of large objects
// - Lambda max memory: 10,240 MB; max /tmp storage: 10,240 MB
// - Lambda max deployment package: 50 MB (zipped) or 250 MB (unzipped)
// - For large processing code+dependencies: use container image (up to 10 GB)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Lambda-backed API (Python), P99 latency requirement: <500ms
// Problem: first request after idle period takes 3-4 seconds (cold start)
// Root cause: Lambda initializes the execution environment on first invocation
//
// Question: What causes Lambda cold starts?
// What are the solutions and their tradeoffs?
// What is Provisioned Concurrency and what does it cost?
// What is Lambda SnapStart and which runtimes support it?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// COLD START ANATOMY:
// When no warm Lambda environment exists:
// 1. Download code package or container image
// 2. Start execution environment (micro-VM)
// 3. Initialize runtime (Python/Node/Java runtime startup)
// 4. Run INIT code (imports, connections, global variable initialization)
// 5. Execute handler function
// Steps 1-4 = cold start duration (hundreds of ms to seconds)
// Step 5 = warm invocation time (milliseconds)
//
// SOLUTION 1: Provisioned Concurrency
//   - Pre-warm N Lambda execution environments at all times
//   - These environments are ALWAYS READY — no cold start ever occurs
//   - Applied to a Lambda ALIAS or specific VERSION (not $LATEST)
//   - Cost: charged per provisioned environment-hour + normal invocation costs
//   - Auto-scaling available: Application Auto Scaling can adjust provisioned count
//     based on schedule or utilization metrics
//   - Best for: latency-critical production APIs
//
// SOLUTION 2: Lambda SnapStart (Java only)
//   - AWS creates a snapshot of the initialized Lambda environment (after INIT phase)
//   - On cold start: restores from snapshot instead of re-initializing from scratch
//   - Reduces cold start from seconds to sub-second for Java
//   - Supported runtimes: Java 11 and later (Corretto)
//   - SnapStart snapshots are version-specific (not $LATEST)
//   - Caveat: unique state per execution (network connections, random seeds) must
//     be re-initialized using beforeCheckpoint/afterRestore lifecycle hooks
//
// SOLUTION 3: Minimize Cold Start Time (code optimization)
//   - Reduce package size: include only necessary libraries
//   - Lazy initialization: defer expensive init (DB connections) to first actual need
//   - Use arm64 (Graviton2) architecture: faster init + cheaper
//   - Avoid compiled languages if warm latency is acceptable (Python/Node start faster)
//   - Move expensive init outside handler: runs once per environment lifetime
//
// SOLUTION 4: Keep-Warm (not recommended)
//   - Schedule EventBridge rule to ping Lambda every 5 minutes
//   - Keeps execution environment warm
//   - Unreliable: multiple concurrent invocations still trigger cold starts
//   - Not a proper solution — use Provisioned Concurrency instead
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Provisioned Concurrency = PRE-WARMED environments (eliminates cold starts)
// - Reserved Concurrency = MAXIMUM concurrent executions (does NOT prevent cold starts)
// - SnapStart: Java only, snapshot-based, must handle stateful initialization in hooks
// - Cold starts more likely: after idle periods, during traffic spikes, after deployments
// - Lambda allocated memory affects cold start time (more memory = faster init)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Lambda needs to connect to RDS PostgreSQL in VPC private subnet.
// Developer configured Lambda with VPC settings (same VPC/subnet as RDS).
// Problem 1: Lambda still cannot connect to RDS (timeout)
// Problem 2: Lambda in VPC can no longer call internet APIs
//
// Question: What additional networking configurations are needed?
// Why does placing Lambda in a VPC lose internet access?
// What is the recommended solution for Lambda-to-RDS connectivity at scale?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// PROBLEM 1 FIX: Configure Security Groups for Lambda → RDS communication
//
// SECURITY GROUP CONFIGURATION:
// Lambda Security Group (lambda-sg):
//   - Outbound: Allow TCP 5432 to rds-sg (or 0.0.0.0/0 for all outbound)
//
// RDS Security Group (rds-sg):
//   - Inbound: Allow TCP 5432 FROM lambda-sg (not from 0.0.0.0/0)
//   - This allows ONLY Lambda (with lambda-sg) to connect to RDS
//
// LAMBDA VPC CONFIGURATION:
//   - VPC: same VPC as RDS
//   - Subnets: private subnets (SAME or routable to RDS subnet)
//   - Security Group: lambda-sg (with outbound to rds-sg)
//   - Lambda execution role: must have ec2:CreateNetworkInterface,
//     ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces, ec2:DeleteNetworkInterface permissions
//     (or attach AWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole managed policy)
//
// PROBLEM 2 FIX: Internet access for Lambda in VPC
// When Lambda is in a VPC:
//   - It behaves like any VPC resource — no automatic internet access
//   - Public subnets: Lambda still cannot access internet (Lambda ENIs have no public IP)
//   - Private subnets + NAT Gateway: Lambda CAN access internet
// Fix: place Lambda in PRIVATE subnet + ensure private subnet has route to NAT Gateway
//
// SCALE ISSUE: Lambda + RDS connection limits
// - Lambda can have 1000s of concurrent executions
// - Each Lambda invocation opens a new RDS connection
// - RDS has connection limits (e.g., t3.medium: ~170 connections)
// - Result: connection pool exhausted → 'too many connections' error
//
// SOLUTION: RDS Proxy
//   - Managed connection pooler sitting between Lambda and RDS
//   - Lambda connects to Proxy (fast, low-latency endpoint)
//   - Proxy maintains connection pool to RDS (multiplexes many Lambda connections)
//   - Reduces RDS connections from N Lambda functions to small pool
//   - Also: automatic failover for Multi-AZ RDS (faster than RDS failover)
//   - Cost: additional hourly charge per RDS instance
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Lambda in VPC: must configure Security Groups on BOTH Lambda and RDS sides
// - Lambda in public subnet still cannot reach internet (no public IP on ENI)
// - For internet from Lambda in VPC: use NAT Gateway in public subnet
// - RDS Proxy: solves connection exhaustion for serverless → RDS architectures
// - Lambda execution role needs VPC ENI permissions (AWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole)

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Account-level Lambda concurrency limit: 1,000 (default)
// Payment Lambda: consuming ~950 concurrent executions during peak
// Auth Lambda: being throttled with 0 available concurrency (starved)
// Both functions are critical production workloads
//
// Question: How does Lambda concurrency work?
// What is the difference between reserved and provisioned concurrency?
// How do you prevent one function from starving others?
// What happens to throttled Lambda invocations?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// LAMBDA CONCURRENCY CONCEPTS:
//
// ACCOUNT-LEVEL CONCURRENCY POOL:
//   - Default: 1,000 concurrent Lambda executions per account per region
//   - Can request increase up to tens of thousands via Service Quotas
//   - All functions in the account SHARE this pool by default
//
// RESERVED CONCURRENCY:
//   - Sets BOTH a minimum AND maximum for a specific function
//   - Minimum: the reserved amount is always available for this function
//     (cannot be consumed by other functions)
//   - Maximum: the function cannot exceed this concurrency limit
//     (excess requests are THROTTLED)
//   - Does NOT pre-warm environments (cold starts still possible)
//   - Cost: FREE (no charge for reserved concurrency configuration)
//   - Unreserved pool = account limit - sum of all reserved concurrency
//
// FIX FOR THE SCENARIO:
//   Set reserved concurrency for critical functions:
//   - Auth Lambda: Reserve 200 concurrent executions
//   - Payment Lambda: Reserve 700 concurrent executions
//   - Remaining 100: available for other functions
//   Result: Auth Lambda can no longer be starved by Payment Lambda
//
// PROVISIONED CONCURRENCY (different purpose):
//   - Pre-warms execution environments (eliminates cold starts)
//   - Applied to a specific version or alias
//   - Costs money: charged per provisioned environment-hour
//   - Does NOT affect whether throttling occurs (use Reserved Concurrency for that)
//
// THROTTLING BEHAVIOR BY INVOCATION TYPE:
//
// SYNCHRONOUS invocations (API Gateway → Lambda):
//   - Throttled: returns 429 TooManyRequestsException immediately
//   - Caller must implement retry with exponential backoff
//   - No automatic retry by Lambda
//
// ASYNCHRONOUS invocations (S3, SNS, EventBridge → Lambda):
//   - Throttled: event goes back to Lambda's async retry queue
//   - Lambda retries for up to 6 hours (with exponential backoff)
//   - Can configure DLQ or Lambda Destination for failed events
//
// EVENT SOURCE MAPPING (SQS, Kinesis → Lambda):
//   - Throttled: Lambda pauses reading from the source
//   - SQS messages remain in queue (visibility timeout eventually returns them)
//   - Kinesis: iterator stays at current position until Lambda unthrottles
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Reserved Concurrency: guarantees AND caps a function's concurrency
// - Provisioned Concurrency: pre-warms environments (no cold start)
// - Setting Reserved = 0 effectively DISABLES a function (all requests throttled)
// - Throttling behavior differs for sync vs async invocations
// - Request concurrency limit increase via Service Quotas console

starterCode:
// Three Lambda event source integrations:
// 1. SQS Standard Queue: order processing (each order is independent)
// 2. Kinesis Data Stream: clickstream analytics (ordered per user)
// 3. DynamoDB Stream: change data capture (ordered per item)
//
// Question: How does Lambda batch processing work for each?
// What happens when one message/record in a batch fails?
// What is 'bisect on error' and when should you use it?
// How do you handle poison pill messages?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// SQS EVENT SOURCE MAPPING:
//   - Lambda polls SQS and receives a BATCH of messages (1-10,000 per batch)
//   - Messages in a batch are INDEPENDENT (not ordered)
//   - On SUCCESS: Lambda deletes entire batch from SQS
//   - On FAILURE (entire function fails): entire batch returned to queue
//     → Messages become visible again after visibility timeout
//   - PARTIAL BATCH FAILURE support:
//     * Report specific message IDs as failures in the response
//     * Only failed messages returned to queue; successful ones deleted
//     * Enable via 'Report Batch Item Failures' function response type
//   - Dead Letter Queue (DLQ): messages that fail N times moved to DLQ
//   - Batch window: collect messages for up to 5 minutes before triggering Lambda
//
// KINESIS DATA STREAM EVENT SOURCE MAPPING:
//   - Lambda reads from each SHARD sequentially (maintains order)
//   - Batch of records per invocation (1-10,000 per batch)
//   - On FAILURE: Lambda retries the SAME BATCH until:
//     * Success, OR
//     * Records expire from stream (24hr-7days retention), OR
//     * Max retry attempts reached
//   - BLOCKED SHARD: failure blocks ALL subsequent records in that shard
//   - BISECT ON ERROR: if batch fails, Lambda splits batch in half and retries
//     each half — identifies the poisonous record(s) faster
//   - Destination on failure: send failed records to SQS DLQ or S3
//
// DYNAMODB STREAMS EVENT SOURCE MAPPING:
//   - Similar to Kinesis: ordered per partition key
//   - Reads INSERT, MODIFY, REMOVE events
//   - On failure: blocks the shard (same as Kinesis)
//   - BISECT ON ERROR: also available (works same as Kinesis)
//   - Can filter events by event type (INSERT only, MODIFY only, etc.)
//   - Destination on failure: SQS or SNS for failed records
//
// HANDLING POISON PILL MESSAGES:
// Strategy 1: Bisect on Error (find and isolate the bad record)
// Strategy 2: Max Retry Attempts: set low retry count → failed records go to DLQ faster
// Strategy 3: Partial Batch Response for SQS: report failures individually
// Strategy 4: Filter criteria: skip records that match bad patterns before Lambda
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - SQS: independent messages, partial batch failures supported
// - Kinesis/DynamoDB Streams: ordered shards, failure BLOCKS the shard
// - Bisect on Error: available for Kinesis and DynamoDB Streams
// - SQS visibility timeout must be >= Lambda timeout * 6 (to avoid double processing)
// - SQS batch size: 1-10,000; Kinesis batch size: 1-10,000 per shard

starterCode:
// Problem:
// 5 Lambda functions, each bundling the same 150 MB library set
// Total storage: 750 MB of duplicated dependencies
// Deployment slow: uploading 150 MB for every code change
// Same libraries need to be updated across 5 functions when a library upgrades
//
// Question: What is a Lambda Layer?
// How does it solve the duplicate library problem?
// What are the size limits and how many layers can a function use?
// What else can Lambda Layers be used for beyond sharing libraries?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// LAMBDA LAYER CONCEPT:
//   - A ZIP archive packaged separately from the function code
//   - Extracted to /opt directory in the Lambda execution environment
//   - Functions reference layers by ARN — no need to bundle the code
//   - Multiple functions share the SAME layer — no duplication
//   - Layers are versioned (immutable; new version on each update)
//
// HOW IT SOLVES THE PROBLEM:
//   Before: 5 functions × 150 MB = 750 MB
//   After:  1 layer (150 MB) + 5 small function packages (~5 MB each) = 175 MB
//   Deployment: only upload the small function package on code changes
//   Library update: update layer once → update function references to new version
//
// LAYER STRUCTURE:
//   layer.zip
//   └── python/                    (for Python runtimes)
//       └── lib/python3.11/site-packages/
//           ├── numpy/
//           └── pandas/
//   OR for Node.js:
//   └── nodejs/node_modules/
//
// SIZE LIMITS:
//   - Max layer size: 250 MB unzipped (per layer)
//   - Max 5 layers per Lambda function
//   - Total unzipped deployment package + layers: 250 MB limit
//   - For larger packages: use container images (up to 10 GB)
//
// USE CASES BEYOND LIBRARIES:
// 1. Shared utility code (logging, error handling modules)
// 2. Custom runtimes: run Lambda in unsupported languages (Go 1.x, Ruby custom, C++)
//    by including a bootstrap executable in the layer
// 3. Configuration and data files (ML model weights, static lookup tables)
// 4. AWS Lambda Powertools layer: structured logging, tracing, metrics
//    (AWS provides this as a public layer ARN you can directly reference)
//
// LAYER SHARING:
//   - Layers can be shared within an account or made PUBLIC
//   - Public layers: accessible by any AWS account (community libraries)
//   - Layer permissions managed via resource-based policies
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Max 5 layers per function; max 250 MB total unzipped (function + all layers)
// - Layers are extracted to /opt — runtime determines actual path within /opt
// - Layer versioning is immutable — update creates a new version (old unchanged)
// - Container images are the alternative for >250 MB total package size
// - Custom runtime requires a bootstrap executable in the layer root

starterCode:
// Three configuration types with different requirements:
// 1. Non-sensitive DB hostname: changes per environment, simple key-value
// 2. Feature flags: change frequently (no redeployment on change)
// 3. DB password: sensitive, must auto-rotate every 30 days, encrypted
//
// Question: Which configuration storage mechanism is appropriate for each?
// What are the performance implications of each choice?
// When should you NOT use environment variables for configuration?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// OPTION 1: Lambda Environment Variables
//   → Best for: Non-sensitive DB hostname (scenario 1)
//   - Key-value pairs baked into the Lambda configuration
//   - Available as process environment variables (os.environ in Python)
//   - Injected at function initialization — no API call overhead at runtime
//   - Changing values requires updating the function configuration (triggers new deployment)
//   - Encrypted at rest with Lambda-managed KMS key (or your own CMK)
//   - NOT suitable for: secrets (visible in Lambda console), frequently changing values
//   - Limit: max 4 KB total environment variables
//
// OPTION 2: SSM Parameter Store
//   → Best for: Feature flags (scenario 2)
//   - Central key-value store accessed via AWS SDK/CLI
//   - Changes take effect immediately without Lambda redeployment
//   - Standard tier: free, max 4,096 characters per value
//   - Advanced tier: $0.05/parameter/month, larger values, TTL, policies
//   - SecureString type: encrypted with KMS
//   - Lambda execution role needs ssm:GetParameter permission
//   - Best practice: cache parameters in Lambda memory and refresh periodically
//     to avoid SSM API throttling (40 TPS standard, 100 TPS advanced)
//   - Does NOT support automatic rotation natively
//
// OPTION 3: AWS Secrets Manager
//   → Best for: DB password with auto-rotation (scenario 3)
//   - Designed specifically for secrets management
//   - Native automatic rotation: built-in rotation for RDS, Redshift, DocumentDB
//   - Custom rotation: Lambda function triggered on schedule for other secrets
//   - Cross-account secret sharing
//   - Lambda execution role needs secretsmanager:GetSecretValue permission
//   - Cost: $0.40/secret/month + $0.05 per 10,000 API calls
//   - Lambda SDK and Secrets Manager caching client: cache secrets in memory,
//     only fetches from service if TTL expires or secret version changes
//
// PERFORMANCE CONSIDERATION:
//   - Environment vars: zero-latency (in memory, no API call)
//   - Parameter Store: single API call per invocation (~10ms) unless cached
//   - Secrets Manager: single API call per invocation (~10ms) unless cached
//   - Both: use AWS Parameter Store and Secrets Manager caching layers to
//     minimize API calls (Lambda Powertools handles this elegantly)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Env vars: simple, fast, not for secrets, require redeploy to change
// - Parameter Store: free (standard), no auto-rotation, good for feature flags
// - Secrets Manager: paid, automatic rotation, designed for credentials
// - Always cache external config in Lambda global scope (persists across warm invocations)
// - Never log secrets: mask them in CloudWatch Logs

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Lambda function needs:
// - dynamodb:PutItem on a specific table
// - sns:Publish on a specific topic
// - logs:CreateLogGroup, logs:CreateLogStream, logs:PutLogEvents
//
// Developer's approach: created IAM User, stored access keys in env vars
// Result: Access Denied on DynamoDB
//
// Question: What is the CORRECT mechanism for granting Lambda permissions?
// Why is the IAM User + env vars approach wrong?
// What is automatically required for ALL Lambda functions?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// CORRECT MECHANISM: IAM Execution Role
//
// WHAT IS AN EXECUTION ROLE:
//   - An IAM ROLE (not a User) that Lambda ASSUMES during execution
//   - Trust policy: allows lambda.amazonaws.com to assume the role
//   - Permission policies: define what AWS services the Lambda can call
//   - Lambda automatically fetches temporary credentials from the Execution Role
//   - Credentials are injected via IMDS — no code changes needed
//   - AWS SDK automatically discovers and uses these credentials
//
// WHY IAM USER + ENV VARS IS WRONG:
//   - Long-term credentials: never expire, high risk if compromised
//   - Visible in Lambda console: env vars visible to anyone with console access
//   - Operational burden: manual rotation required
//   - Anti-pattern: violates AWS security best practices
//
// SETTING UP THE EXECUTION ROLE:
// 1. Create IAM Role with Trust Policy:
//    Principal: lambda.amazonaws.com
//    Action: sts:AssumeRole
// 2. Attach permission policies:
//    - Custom policy: Allow dynamodb:PutItem on arn:aws:dynamodb:::table/orders
//    - Custom policy: Allow sns:Publish on arn:aws:sns:::order-notifications
//    - AWS Managed: AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole
//      (grants: logs:CreateLogGroup, logs:PutLogEvents, logs:CreateLogStream)
// 3. Assign Execution Role when creating/updating Lambda function
//
// CLOUDWATCH LOGS — REQUIRED FOR ALL LAMBDA:
//   - AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole: AWS managed policy
//   - Grants CloudWatch Logs permissions needed for Lambda to write logs
//   - If missing: Lambda runs but logs are silently lost
//   - Every Lambda function should have this policy attached
//
// EXECUTION ROLE FOR VPC LAMBDA:
//   - AWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole (extends Basic with ENI permissions)
//   - Adds: ec2:CreateNetworkInterface, ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Lambda uses Execution Role (IAM Role), NOT IAM User credentials
// - Trust policy must have lambda.amazonaws.com as Principal
// - AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole: minimum required for all Lambda functions
// - Never store AWS credentials in Lambda environment variables
// - One Execution Role per function (principle of least privilege per function)

starterCode:
// Social media app access patterns:
// 1. Get all posts by userId
// 2. Get specific post by userId + postId
// 3. Get all posts by userId, sorted newest first
// 4. Get all comments for a specific postId
//
// Question: What partition key and sort key should be used?
// Can a single table design support all four access patterns?
// What is the key design rule for DynamoDB: design for access patterns?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// DYNAMODB KEY DESIGN PRINCIPLES:
//
// PRIMARY KEY — COMPOSITE KEY DESIGN:
//   Partition Key (PK): userId       → groups all posts by a user together
//   Sort Key (SK):      postId#timestamp  → enables range queries within a user's posts
//
// ACCESS PATTERN MAPPING:
// 1. Get all posts by userId:
//    → Query(PK = userId) — all items in partition returned
//
// 2. Get specific post by userId + postId:
//    → GetItem(PK = userId, SK = postId) — O(1) lookup
//
// 3. Get posts by userId sorted newest first:
//    → Query(PK = userId, SortKeyCondition = begins_with('POST#'),
//            ScanIndexForward = false) — reverse sort order
//    → Store timestamp as ISO8601 string in SK for lexicographic sort
//    → Or use inverted timestamps (MaxLong - epoch) for natural newest-first order
//
// 4. Get all comments for a postId:
//    → This is a NEW access pattern: query by postId (not userId)
//    → Two options:
//      a) Store comments in SAME table with PK=postId, SK='COMMENT#timestamp'
//         (Single Table Design pattern using prefixes to distinguish entity types)
//      b) Create Global Secondary Index (GSI) on postId attribute
//
// SINGLE TABLE DESIGN PATTERN:
//   Entity Type | PK          | SK                    | Data
//   Post        | USER#userId | POST#timestamp#postId | title, content
//   Comment     | POST#postId | COMMENT#timestamp     | text, authorId
//
//   Prefixes (USER#, POST#, COMMENT#) prevent key collisions between entity types
//
// KEY DYNAMODB DESIGN RULES:
// 1. Design for access patterns FIRST — identify all reads before creating table
// 2. Partition key should have HIGH cardinality (many unique values) for even distribution
// 3. Avoid 'hot partitions': userId as PK is fine (spread across many users)
// 4. Sort key enables range queries within a partition
// 5. GSIs for access patterns not served by primary key
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Partition key alone: use for Get by exact value (no range)
// - Partition key + Sort key: use for range queries within a partition
// - High cardinality partition key is critical for even load distribution
// - Single Table Design: multiple entity types in one table using key prefixes
// - Query is efficient (uses index); Scan is inefficient (reads entire table)

starterCode:
// DynamoDB table: Orders
// Primary Key: OrderId (partition key only)
// Attributes: status, createdAt, customerId, totalAmount
//
// Problematic query: Scan entire table, filter where status='PENDING'
// Problem: 45-second scans, expensive, throttling other reads
// Target: fast queries (<100ms) for 'all pending orders by date'
//
// Question: What is the correct solution to avoid the full table scan?
// How do you design a GSI to serve this access pattern?
// What is the difference between LSI and GSI?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: Global Secondary Index (GSI)
//
// NAIVE GSI DESIGN (has hot partition problem):
//   GSI Partition Key: status
//   GSI Sort Key: createdAt
//   Query: GSI(PK='PENDING', SortKey > '2024-01-01', ScanIndexForward=false)
//   Problem: 'PENDING' partition may have millions of items → hot partition
//
// BETTER GSI DESIGN (with partition key spreading):
//   Use composite key with shard suffix to distribute hot partitions:
//   GSI Partition Key: status#shard  (e.g., 'PENDING#1' through 'PENDING#10')
//   GSI Sort Key: createdAt
//   Write: randomly assign shard number (1-10) when creating order
//   Read: query each shard in parallel (Parallel Scan on GSI)
//   Merge results application-side
//
// STANDARD GSI DESIGN:
//   GSI Partition Key: status
//   GSI Sort Key: createdAt (ISO8601 for lexicographic date sort)
//   Projection: ALL (include all attributes) or KEYS_ONLY or custom
//
// GSI vs LSI:
// Global Secondary Index (GSI):
//   - Partition key DIFFERENT from base table partition key
//   - Can be created at any time (even after table creation)
//   - Has its OWN provisioned throughput separate from base table
//   - Eventually consistent reads only (no strongly consistent)
//   - Can have different partition key and sort key from base table
//   - Limit: up to 20 GSIs per table
//
// Local Secondary Index (LSI):
//   - SAME partition key as base table, DIFFERENT sort key
//   - Must be created AT TABLE CREATION TIME (cannot add later)
//   - Shares throughput with base table
//   - Supports strongly consistent reads
//   - Limit: up to 5 LSIs per table
//   - Use when: same partition key, different sort key needed
//
// FOR THIS SCENARIO:
//   Use GSI (different partition key: status vs OrderId in base table)
//   LSI cannot be used (would need partition key to remain OrderId)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - GSI: different partition key, created anytime, own throughput, eventual consistency
// - LSI: same partition key, created at table creation, shared throughput, strong consistency
// - Scan + FilterExpression: still reads all items, filter happens AFTER read (wastes RCUs)
// - GSI hot partitions: if one PK value has millions of items, consider composite keys
// - GSI projection: ALL = more storage but no fetches; KEYS_ONLY = less storage + fetch

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Banking app: $500 transferred from Account A to Account B.
// Read immediately after write: shows OLD balance (missing $500)
// Cause: DynamoDB consistency model
//
// Question: What are DynamoDB's two read consistency models?
// Why does the default read show the old value?
// How do you configure a read to guarantee seeing the latest write?
// What is the cost difference between the two consistency models?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// DYNAMODB CONSISTENCY MODELS:
//
// EVENTUALLY CONSISTENT READS (default):
//   - May read from any of the 3 AZ replicas
//   - A recently completed write may not have propagated to all replicas yet
//   - Possible to read stale data (the old value before the write)
//   - Cost: 0.5 RCU per 4 KB item (half the cost of strong consistency)
//   - Typically becomes consistent within milliseconds
//   - Appropriate for: non-critical reads where slight staleness is acceptable
//     (product catalog, user profile reads, analytics)
//
// STRONGLY CONSISTENT READS:
//   - DynamoDB ensures the read reflects ALL successful writes before the read
//   - Returns the most up-to-date value
//   - Implemented by reading from the LEADER node (primary write node)
//   - Cost: 1 RCU per 4 KB item (2x the cost of eventual consistency)
//   - Slightly higher latency (must contact leader node)
//   - Only available for GetItem, Query, Scan (not for GSI reads!)
//   - NOT available for Global Tables (multi-region replication) — always eventual
//
// HOW TO CONFIGURE:
//   GetItem: set ConsistentRead = true
//   Query: set ConsistentRead = true
//   Scan: set ConsistentRead = true
//   Note: GSIs ALWAYS use eventual consistency (ConsistentRead is ignored on GSIs)
//
// FOR THE BANKING SCENARIO:
//   Read Account B balance with ConsistentRead = true
//   This guarantees the balance reflects the completed $500 transfer
//
// ADDITIONAL BANKING PATTERN — TRANSACTIONS:
//   For the actual transfer (debit A + credit B atomically):
//   Use DynamoDB Transactions (TransactWriteItems):
//   - Atomic: either both succeed or both fail
//   - Consistent: reads within transaction see committed data
//   - Isolated: concurrent transactions don't interfere
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Default reads are EVENTUALLY consistent (0.5 RCU per 4 KB)
// - ConsistentRead=true: strongly consistent (1 RCU per 4 KB) — 2x cost
// - GSI reads are ALWAYS eventually consistent (no option for strong)
// - Global Tables (multi-region): always eventually consistent
// - For financial applications: strongly consistent reads for balance checks

starterCode:
// Requirement:
// When order status changes to 'SHIPPED' in DynamoDB:
// 1. Trigger Lambda to send shipping email
// 2. Send ALL DynamoDB changes to Elasticsearch for analytics
//
// Question: What is DynamoDB Streams?
// What are the stream view types and when to use each?
// How does Lambda integrate with DynamoDB Streams?
// How do you filter events to process only SHIPPED status changes?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// DYNAMODB STREAMS:
//   - Captures time-ordered sequence of item-level modifications
//   - Retention: 24 hours (items older than 24h are automatically removed)
//   - Each change record contains the modified item and event type (INSERT/MODIFY/REMOVE)
//   - Sharded (similar to Kinesis): ordered per partition key
//   - Max 2 simultaneous stream readers per shard (limit of DynamoDB Streams)
//
// STREAM VIEW TYPES:
//   KEYS_ONLY:   Only the key attributes (PK, SK) of the modified item
//   NEW_IMAGE:   The item after the change (full new state)
//   OLD_IMAGE:   The item before the change (full old state)
//   NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES: Both old and new state (most flexible, highest data transfer)
//   → For this scenario: NEW_IMAGE (need new status = 'SHIPPED')
//   → For Elasticsearch: NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES (track what changed)
//
// LAMBDA + DYNAMODB STREAMS INTEGRATION:
//   - Configure Lambda event source mapping on the stream ARN
//   - Lambda polls the stream for new records
//   - Records come in batches (1-1000 records per batch)
//   - Events are ordered within a shard (partition key)
//   - Lambda receives: eventName (INSERT/MODIFY/REMOVE), newImage, oldImage
//
// EVENT FILTERING (Lambda-side):
//   - Use Lambda Event Source Mapping filter criteria:
//     Filter: eventName = MODIFY AND newImage.status.S = SHIPPED
//   - Only events matching the filter invoke the Lambda function
//   - Reduces Lambda invocations and costs
//
// ARCHITECTURE:
// DynamoDB → Streams → Lambda (shipping notification)
//                   ↘
//                    Kinesis Firehose → Elasticsearch (analytics)
//   OR:
// DynamoDB → Streams → Lambda (CDC handler) → SNS Topic → Lambda (email)
//                                                        → Kinesis Firehose → ES
//
// NOTE ON 2-READER LIMIT:
//   DynamoDB Streams allows max 2 simultaneous GetRecords calls per shard
//   If you need 3+ consumers: use Streams → Lambda → SNS/SQS fan-out pattern
//   OR use Kinesis Data Streams (DynamoDB Kinesis Streaming) — allows multiple consumers
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - DynamoDB Streams: 24-hour retention, ordered within partition, 2 reader limit
// - Stream view types: NEW_IMAGE, OLD_IMAGE, KEYS_ONLY, NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES
// - Lambda filter criteria: filter at source to reduce invocations
// - For >2 stream consumers: fan out via SNS/SQS after first Lambda consumer
// - DynamoDB Kinesis Streaming: alternative to Streams with higher throughput

starterCode:
// Current solution: Lambda scans entire session table every hour, deletes old items
// Problem: expensive full table scan, Lambda costs
// Session data: userId, sessionToken, createdAt, sessionData
// Requirement: automatically delete sessions 24 hours after creation
//
// Question: What is DynamoDB TTL?
// How do you configure it?
// When are items actually deleted after the TTL attribute expires?
// Does TTL count as consumed capacity?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// DYNAMODB TTL (Time To Live):
//   - Built-in feature: DynamoDB automatically deletes items after a specified time
//   - No manual scans, no Lambda needed, no provisioned capacity consumed
//   - Items are deleted asynchronously — not an instant deletion at exactly TTL time
//
// CONFIGURATION:
// 1. Add a TTL attribute to your item (e.g., 'expiresAt')
//    Value: Unix epoch timestamp in SECONDS (not milliseconds)
//    Example: createdAt + 86400 (24 hours in seconds)
//    If createdAt = 1700000000, expiresAt = 1700000000 + 86400 = 1700086400
// 2. Enable TTL on the table:
//    - Console: DynamoDB → Table → Additional Settings → TTL
//    - Specify attribute name: 'expiresAt'
//    - DynamoDB starts monitoring this attribute across all items
//
// DELETION BEHAVIOR:
//   - DynamoDB marks items for deletion when current time > expiresAt
//   - Actual deletion: within 48 hours of expiry (typically within minutes to hours)
//   - NOT guaranteed to delete exactly at the TTL time
//   - Expired items may STILL BE RETURNED by queries/scans for up to 48 hours
//     → Filter client-side: add FilterExpression to exclude items where expiresAt < now()
//   - TTL deletions appear in DynamoDB Streams as DELETE events (for CDC use cases)
//
// CAPACITY CONSUMPTION:
//   - TTL deletions do NOT consume Write Capacity Units (WCUs)
//   - Free capacity for cleanup operations
//   - TTL reads (checking expiresAt) also free
//   - Exception: TTL deletions DO consume Streams read capacity (for CDC consumers)
//
// WHAT THE TTL ATTRIBUTE MUST BE:
//   - Number type (not String)
//   - Unix epoch timestamp in SECONDS (not milliseconds)
//   - Attribute must exist on items to be deleted (items without attribute are ignored)
//   - Attribute value must be in the future to be active (past value = already eligible for deletion)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - TTL = specify epoch timestamp; DynamoDB deletes automatically — no WCU consumed
// - Deletion is NOT instantaneous: may take up to 48 hours after TTL
// - Filter expired items in queries if exact expiry timing matters
// - TTL deletions show in DynamoDB Streams as REMOVE events
// - Unix timestamp must be in SECONDS, not milliseconds

starterCode:
// Scenario:
// Flight seat: seatCount=1, one seat remaining
// User A and User B SIMULTANEOUSLY:
//   1. Both read seatCount=1
//   2. Both calculate new value: 1-1=0
//   3. Both write seatCount=0 (both bookings 'succeed')
// Result: 2 bookings for 1 seat (overselling!)
//
// Question: How do you implement optimistic locking in DynamoDB?
// What is a Condition Expression?
// What error is returned when the condition fails?
// What is the alternative using atomic counters?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// SOLUTION 1: OPTIMISTIC LOCKING WITH CONDITION EXPRESSION
//
// READ-MODIFY-WRITE WITH VERSION CHECK:
// Step 1: Read current seatCount (and optionally a version number)
//         GetItem: returns {seatCount: 1}
// Step 2: Calculate new value: 1 - 1 = 0
// Step 3: Write with Condition Expression:
//         UpdateItem(
//           Key: {flightId: 'FL001'},
//           UpdateExpression: 'SET seatCount = :newCount',
//           ConditionExpression: 'seatCount = :expectedCount AND seatCount > :zero',
//           ExpressionAttributeValues: {
//             ':newCount': 0,
//             ':expectedCount': 1,  // the value we read
//             ':zero': 0
//           }
//         )
//
// OUTCOMES:
// User A writes first:
//   Condition: seatCount = 1 (true) AND seatCount > 0 (true) → SUCCEEDS
//   seatCount becomes 0
//
// User B tries to write second:
//   Condition: seatCount = 1 (FALSE — now it's 0) → FAILS
//   DynamoDB throws: ConditionalCheckFailedException
//   Application catches exception, re-reads seatCount (now 0)
//   Application returns 'no seats available' to User B
//
// CONDITION EXPRESSION USE CASES:
//   - attribute_exists(PK): only update if item exists (prevent ghost writes)
//   - attribute_not_exists(PK): only create if item doesn't exist (prevent duplicates)
//   - seatCount > :zero: ensure count doesn't go negative
//   - version = :expectedVersion: version-based optimistic locking
//
// SOLUTION 2: ATOMIC COUNTER (UPDATE_EXPRESSION with ADD)
//   UpdateItem(
//     UpdateExpression: 'ADD seatCount :decrement',
//     ConditionExpression: 'seatCount > :zero',
//     ExpressionAttributeValues: {':decrement': -1, ':zero': 0}
//   )
//   - DynamoDB atomically decrements seatCount
//   - If seatCount = 0: ConditionExpression fails → ConditionalCheckFailedException
//   - No read needed: atomic at DynamoDB server level
//   - Avoids the read-modify-write race entirely
//   - Best for: simple counters where you don't need the current value before writing
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Condition Expressions are evaluated atomically on the DynamoDB server side
// - ConditionalCheckFailedException: raised when condition fails
// - Optimistic locking: read → write with condition → retry on failure
// - Atomic counter: no read needed, ADD/SET expression evaluated atomically
// - Condition Expressions do NOT consume extra capacity units

starterCode:
// Phase 1 (Beta): Unpredictable traffic, 10-10,000 req/sec bursts
// Phase 2 (Stable): Predictable 5,000 reads/sec + 1,000 writes/sec
//
// Question: What is the difference between On-Demand and Provisioned capacity?
// Which is more cost-effective for each phase?
// What is Auto Scaling for Provisioned mode?
// What are WCUs and RCUs and how do you calculate them?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// ON-DEMAND CAPACITY MODE:
//   - Pay per request: ~$1.25 per million write requests, ~$0.25 per million reads
//   - No capacity planning required: scales instantly to any traffic level
//   - No throttling as long as within account limits
//   - Can double previous peak traffic instantaneously
//   - Best for: unpredictable, spiky, new workloads
//   - Cost: MORE expensive at consistent high volume vs Provisioned
//   → RECOMMENDED for Phase 1 (Beta)
//
// PROVISIONED CAPACITY MODE:
//   - Specify RCUs and WCUs per second
//   - Pay per provisioned RCU/WCU per hour regardless of usage
//   - Cost: ~$0.00013/RCU/hour, ~$0.00065/WCU/hour
//   - If traffic exceeds provisioned capacity: requests are THROTTLED
//   - Best for: predictable, steady workloads
//   → RECOMMENDED for Phase 2 (Stable) with Auto Scaling
//
// WCU AND RCU CALCULATIONS:
// WCU (Write Capacity Unit):
//   - 1 WCU = 1 write per second for items up to 1 KB
//   - 2 KB item write: 2 WCUs; 0.5 KB write: 1 WCU (rounded up)
//   - Transactional writes: 2x WCUs
//
// RCU (Read Capacity Unit):
//   - 1 RCU = 1 strongly consistent read OR 2 eventually consistent reads per second
//   - Per 4 KB of item size
//   - 6 KB item strong read: 2 RCUs; 6 KB eventually consistent: 1 RCU
//   - Transactional reads: 2x RCUs
//
// FOR PHASE 2 CALCULATION (assuming 1 KB items):
//   5,000 reads/sec × 1 RCU each (eventually consistent) = 2,500 RCUs
//   1,000 writes/sec × 1 WCU each = 1,000 WCUs
//
// AUTO SCALING FOR PROVISIONED MODE:
//   - Define min/max capacity and target utilization %
//   - DynamoDB Auto Scaling adjusts provisioned capacity using CloudWatch alarms
//   - Scales OUT: when utilization > target
//   - Scales IN: when utilization < target (with cooldown)
//   - Handles predictable variation but NOT instantaneous spikes (there's lag)
//   - Use Provisioned with Auto Scaling for Phase 2 with fluctuations
//
// COST COMPARISON PHASE 2 (1,000 WCU + 2,500 RCU):
//   Provisioned: ~$0.756/hour (~$546/month)
//   On-Demand: depends on actual request count — often 5-6x more expensive at high load
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - On-Demand: pay per request, no capacity planning, best for unpredictable
// - Provisioned: pay per hour, requires capacity planning, cheaper at steady load
// - Auto Scaling: works with Provisioned to adjust capacity automatically
// - Can switch between modes (once per 24 hours)
// - Burst capacity: DynamoDB retains unused capacity for 5 minutes for bursts

starterCode:
// Atomic purchase flow:
// Operation 1: inventory.availableCount - 1 (fail if count = 0)
// Operation 2: Create new order record
// Operation 3: user.balance - purchaseAmount (fail if balance < amount)
//
// All-or-nothing: if ANY operation fails, ALL must be rolled back.
//
// Question: What DynamoDB API enables atomic multi-item transactions?
// Can you mix reads and writes in a transaction?
// What are the limitations on transactions?
// What is the cost vs non-transactional operations?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// API: TransactWriteItems (write transactions) + TransactGetItems (read transactions)
//
// TRANSACTWRITEITEMS:
//   - Atomic: all succeed or all fail (ACID semantics)
//   - Up to 100 actions per transaction across up to 10 different tables
//   - Action types: Put, Update, Delete, ConditionCheck
//   - ConditionCheck: verify conditions on items WITHOUT writing them
//
// PURCHASE FLOW IMPLEMENTATION:
// TransactWriteItems([
//
//   // Check 1: Verify inventory > 0 (read-only condition, no write)
//   ConditionCheck: {
//     TableName: 'Products',
//     Key: {productId: 'P001'},
//     ConditionExpression: 'availableCount > :zero',
//     ExpressionAttributeValues: {':zero': 0}
//   },
//
//   // Check 2: Verify user has sufficient balance (condition on write)
//   Update: {
//     TableName: 'Users',
//     Key: {userId: 'U001'},
//     UpdateExpression: 'SET balance = balance - :amount',
//     ConditionExpression: 'balance >= :amount',
//     ExpressionAttributeValues: {':amount': 50}
//   },
//
//   // Decrement inventory
//   Update: {
//     TableName: 'Products',
//     Key: {productId: 'P001'},
//     UpdateExpression: 'ADD availableCount :decrement',
//     ExpressionAttributeValues: {':decrement': -1}
//   },
//
//   // Create order record
//   Put: {
//     TableName: 'Orders',
//     Item: {orderId: 'O001', userId: 'U001', productId: 'P001', amount: 50},
//     ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(orderId)'  // prevent duplicate
//   }
//
// ])
//
// TRANSACTION FAILURE MODES:
// - If ANY condition fails: TransactionCanceledException (all changes rolled back)
//   - CancellationReasons list shows WHICH items caused the failure
// - If ANY write fails for other reasons: same, all rolled back
//
// TRANSACTGETITEMS:
//   - Read up to 100 items atomically (consistent snapshot)
//   - All items returned as of the same point in time
//   - More expensive than individual GetItem calls
//
// TRANSACTION COSTS:
//   TransactWriteItems: 2x WCUs per item (vs 1x for regular UpdateItem)
//   TransactGetItems: 2x RCUs per item (vs 1x for regular GetItem)
//
// LIMITATIONS:
//   - Max 100 items per transaction
//   - Max 4 MB of data per transaction
//   - A transaction cannot read an item it is also writing
//   - No cross-account transactions
//   - No transactions across DynamoDB Global Tables replicas
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - TransactWriteItems = all-or-nothing writes across multiple tables
// - ConditionCheck: verify without writing (use for precondition checks)
// - TransactionCanceledException: check CancellationReasons for which item failed
// - Cost: 2x WCU/RCU vs non-transactional
// - 100 item limit per transaction

starterCode:
// Three API requirements:
// 1. CRUD web API: custom auth, per-client usage plans, API key tracking, AWS WAF
// 2. Real-time chat: server pushes messages to clients proactively
// 3. High-throughput mobile backend: lowest cost/latency, JWT auth only
//
// Question: What are the three API Gateway types?
// Which is best for each scenario?
// What key features are exclusive to REST API?
// What is the price difference between REST and HTTP API?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// API GATEWAY TYPES:
//
// 1. REST API:
//   - Full-featured HTTP API for traditional request/response patterns
//   - Features: usage plans, API keys, per-method throttling, request/response
//     transformation, AWS WAF integration, caching, edge-optimized (CloudFront)
//   - Supports: Lambda, HTTP backends, Mock integration, AWS services directly
//   - Cost: ~$3.50 per million API calls
//   - Auth: Lambda authorizer, Cognito User Pool, IAM, API key
//   → Best for Scenario 1 (CRUD with usage plans, AWS WAF)
//
// 2. HTTP API (newer, cheaper):
//   - Simplified, lower-latency API for Lambda and HTTP backends
//   - Features: OIDC/JWT authorization, CORS, Lambda proxy, private integrations
//   - MISSING vs REST: no usage plans, no API keys, no WAF integration,
//     no request/response transformation, no edge-optimized, no caching
//   - Cost: ~$1.00 per million API calls (70% cheaper than REST)
//   - Auth: JWT/OIDC natively, Lambda authorizer (no API keys)
//   → Best for Scenario 3 (high throughput, JWT auth, lowest cost)
//
// 3. WebSocket API:
//   - Persistent bidirectional connections
//   - Server can PUSH messages to connected clients via Callback URL
//   - Client connects once (WebSocket handshake), then exchanges messages
//   - Connection management: $connect, $disconnect, $default routes
//   - Lambda receives messages, can call back to client using:
//     POST https://execute-api.region.amazonaws.com/{stage}/@connections/{connectionId}
//   - Cost: $0.80/million messages + $0.25/million connection minutes
//   → Best for Scenario 2 (real-time chat with server push)
//
// FEATURE COMPARISON:
// Feature              | REST API | HTTP API | WebSocket
// Usage Plans/API Keys |   YES    |    NO    |    NO
// AWS WAF Integration  |   YES    |    NO    |    NO
// Response Caching     |   YES    |    NO    |    NO
// Request Transform    |   YES    |    NO    |    NO
// JWT/OIDC Auth        |   YES    |   YES    |    NO
// Lambda Auth          |   YES    |   YES    |   YES
// Server Push          |   NO     |    NO    |   YES
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - REST API: most features, highest cost — use when you need WAF, usage plans, caching
// - HTTP API: 70% cheaper, JWT native, use when you just need Lambda/HTTP proxy
// - WebSocket API: real-time bidirectional, server can push, persistent connections
// - HTTP API is NOT HTTP/2 — it's still HTTP/1.1, just a simpler REST API Gateway

starterCode:
// Three authorization scenarios:
// 1. Third-party partner API: custom OAuth tokens from non-AWS IdP
// 2. Mobile app: email/password login, JWT tokens, auto-validated by API Gateway
// 3. Internal service-to-service API: caller must be an AWS IAM role
//
// Question: Which API Gateway authorization type suits each scenario?
// What does each authorizer check and how does it work?
// Where are authorization results cached?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// SCENARIO 1 (Custom OAuth tokens from third-party IdP): → Lambda Authorizer
//   - Lambda function receives the token from request header/query string
//   - Lambda validates the token against the external IdP (verify signature, expiry)
//   - Lambda returns an IAM policy (Allow or Deny) + optional context
//   - API Gateway evaluates the returned policy to allow or deny the request
//   - Two types:
//     * Token-type: receives bearer token from Authorization header
//     * Request-type: receives all request headers, query strings, method, path
//   - Authorization result is CACHED by API Gateway (TTL: 0-3600 sec, default 300s)
//   - Best for: custom token validation, external IdP, third-party auth, custom logic
//
// SCENARIO 2 (Mobile app, JWT from Cognito): → Cognito User Pool Authorizer
//   - API Gateway natively validates JWTs issued by Cognito User Pool
//   - Validates: token signature, expiry, issuer, audience claims
//   - No Lambda needed — validation built into API Gateway
//   - After Cognito validates: user attributes available in request context
//   - Best for: user authentication with Cognito User Pools, no custom logic needed
//   - Note: HTTP API supports JWT authorizer for any OIDC-compliant IdP (not just Cognito)
//
// SCENARIO 3 (Internal service-to-service, IAM roles): → IAM Authorization
//   - Callers sign requests using AWS SigV4 (derived from IAM credentials)
//   - API Gateway validates the SigV4 signature + checks IAM policy
//   - Caller needs execute-api:Invoke permission on the API ARN
//   - Best for: AWS service-to-service calls, internal APIs, machine-to-machine
//   - Cross-account: can use Resource-Based Policy on API Gateway to allow other accounts
//
// COMPARISON TABLE:
// Method          | Who uses it        | Custom logic | Caching
// Lambda Auth     | External/custom    | YES          | YES (API GW caches result)
// Cognito Auth    | End users (Cognito)| NO           | YES
// IAM Auth        | AWS services/users | NO           | N/A (AWS validates)
// API Key         | Partner systems    | NO           | N/A
//
// LAMBDA AUTHORIZER CACHING:
//   - Result cached by the token value (or request attributes for request-type)
//   - Subsequent requests with same token use cached policy (no Lambda invocation)
//   - TTL configurable (0 = no cache; 3600 = max 1 hour)
//   - Reduces Lambda invocations and latency
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Lambda Authorizer: custom validation, returns IAM policy, result cached
// - Cognito User Pool: JWT validation built-in, no Lambda needed
// - IAM Auth: SigV4 signing, for AWS service principals and IAM users
// - Cognito Identity Pool vs User Pool: User Pool = authentication (who are you),
//   Identity Pool = authorization (exchange Cognito token for AWS credentials)

starterCode:
// Requirements:
// - Free tier: 100 req/min, burst 200
// - Premium tier: 1,000 req/min, burst 2,000
// - All customers combined: max 500 req/sec on any single method
// - Track consumption per customer
//
// Question: How do API keys, usage plans, and throttling work together?
// What is the difference between stage-level and method-level throttling?
// What are the three throttling layers in API Gateway?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// ARCHITECTURE:
// API Key → Usage Plan → Stage → API
//
// STEP 1: Create API Keys
//   - One API key per customer (identifies the consumer)
//   - Customer includes key in request: x-api-key header
//   - API Gateway validates the key before processing
//
// STEP 2: Create Usage Plans
//   Free Tier Usage Plan:
//   - Throttle: Rate = 100 requests/second (approx), Burst = 200
//   - Quota: 3,000 requests/day (optional daily/weekly/monthly limit)
//   - Associate with API stage
//
//   Premium Usage Plan:
//   - Throttle: Rate = 1,000 requests/second, Burst = 2,000
//   - Quota: 30,000 requests/day
//   - Associate with same or different API stage
//
// STEP 3: Associate API Keys with Usage Plans
//   - Each customer's API key is added to the appropriate usage plan
//   - API Gateway enforces limits per API key
//
// THREE THROTTLING LAYERS (applied in this order):
//
// Layer 1 — Account-Level (AWS default):
//   - 10,000 requests/second per region (soft limit, can increase)
//   - 5,000 burst limit
//   - Applies to ALL API stages in the account
//
// Layer 2 — Stage-Level Throttling:
//   - Set per stage (e.g., 'prod' stage: 500 req/sec, burst 1,000)
//   - Applies to ALL methods in that stage collectively
//   → Set stage-level to 500 req/sec for the '500 req/sec per method' requirement
//
// Layer 3 — Method-Level Throttling:
//   - Override throttling for specific methods/resources within a stage
//   - E.g., GET /search: 100 req/sec (expensive operation, limit more)
//   - E.g., POST /order: 500 req/sec (critical path, allow higher)
//
// THROTTLING RESPONSE:
//   - When limits exceeded: API Gateway returns 429 Too Many Requests
//   - Response body: {"message": "Too Many Requests"}
//   - Client should implement exponential backoff retry
//
// MONITORING:
//   - CloudWatch metrics per API key + usage plan
//   - CacheHitCount, CacheMissCount, Count (total requests), 4XXError, 5XXError
//   - X-Ray for distributed tracing through API Gateway → Lambda
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - API Keys: identify consumers (NOT authentication — use in addition to auth)
// - Usage Plans: throttle rates + quotas per API key
// - Stage throttling: applies to all methods combined in a stage
// - Method throttling: per-method override (most specific wins)
// - 429 Too Many Requests: the throttling response code

starterCode:
// Product catalog API:
// - 50,000 req/min, 99% for same 100 products
// - Currently: every request invokes Lambda + DynamoDB read
// - Data freshness: cache can be up to 1 hour stale
// - Problem: high Lambda + DynamoDB costs
//
// Question: How does API Gateway caching work?
// How do you configure TTL and cache invalidation?
// How do you ensure different users get their own cached response (cache keys)?
// What is the cost of enabling API Gateway cache?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// API GATEWAY RESPONSE CACHING:
//   - Configured at the STAGE level (not method level, but can override per method)
//   - API Gateway stores responses and returns them for identical cache-key requests
//   - Backend Lambda is NOT invoked for cache hits — direct response from cache
//   - Cache sizes: 0.5 GB to 237 GB (choose based on number of unique responses)
//   - Cost: hourly charge per GB of cache size (0.5 GB = ~$0.02/hour)
//
// CONFIGURATION:
//   Stage Settings → Caching:
//   - Enable cache: ON
//   - Cache TTL: 3600 seconds (1 hour for this scenario)
//   - Cache capacity: choose based on unique response count
//   - Default: caches based on the resource path (not query strings/headers)
//
// CACHE KEY CUSTOMIZATION:
//   Default cache key: HTTP method + resource path
//   Example: GET /products/P001 → cached separately from GET /products/P002
//
//   Adding query strings to cache key:
//   - Enable: 'Cache query string parameters'
//   - GET /products?category=electronics is DIFFERENT cache key from /products?category=books
//
//   Adding headers to cache key:
//   - Can include specific headers in cache key
//   - Example: include 'Accept-Language' to cache different responses per language
//   - Include 'Authorization' header if users need personalized responses
//     (WARNING: each unique user would create a unique cache entry → defeats purpose)
//
// CACHE INVALIDATION:
//   Manual: invalidate entire cache in console/CLI
//   Programmatic: client sends Cache-Control: max-age=0 header
//   - API must be configured to 'Require authorization' for client invalidation
//   - Without 'Require authorization': any client can bypass cache
//
// BEST PRACTICES FOR THIS SCENARIO:
//   TTL: 3600 seconds (1 hour matches data freshness requirement)
//   Cache key: path only (GET /products/{productId} — productId in path)
//   No Authorization in cache key (products are public data)
//   Result: same product response served to all users from cache
//   Cost reduction: ~99% fewer Lambda invocations (only 100 unique products cached)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - API GW cache only for REST API (not HTTP API — which has no caching)
// - Cache is per stage: dev/staging/prod can have different TTLs
// - Cache key = path + optionally query strings + optionally headers
// - Including Authorization in cache key = per-user cache (limited benefit)
// - Cache-Control: max-age=0 bypasses cache if 'honor client Cache-Control' enabled

starterCode:
// React app at: https://app.example.com
// API at: https://api.example.com (different domain)
// Browser error: CORS policy blocked
// Postman works fine (no CORS error in non-browser clients)
//
// Question: What is CORS and why does the browser enforce it?
// What is a preflight request?
// How do you enable CORS in API Gateway?
// What Lambda changes are needed to return CORS headers?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// WHAT IS CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing):
//   - Browser security feature that restricts cross-origin HTTP requests
//   - A 'cross-origin' request: different domain, port, or protocol
//   - app.example.com calling api.example.com = different origin → CORS applies
//   - Browser enforces CORS; non-browser clients (Postman, curl) do NOT enforce it
//   - Server must explicitly ALLOW the requesting origin via response headers
//
// PREFLIGHT REQUEST:
//   - Before non-simple requests (POST/PUT/DELETE or custom headers),
//     browser sends an OPTIONS preflight request to check if CORS is allowed
//   - Preflight headers from browser:
//     Origin: https://app.example.com
//     Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
//     Access-Control-Request-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization
//   - Server must respond to OPTIONS with CORS approval headers
//
// ENABLING CORS IN API GATEWAY:
//
// For REST API (simple method):
//   1. Select resource in console → Actions → Enable CORS
//   2. API Gateway creates OPTIONS method with Mock integration
//   3. OPTIONS response includes required headers automatically
//   4. Set allowed origins, methods, headers in the CORS dialog
//
// Required response headers for preflight (OPTIONS):
//   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com (or * for all)
//   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET,POST,PUT,DELETE,OPTIONS
//   Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type,Authorization
//   Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400 (cache preflight for 24 hours)
//
// FOR LAMBDA PROXY INTEGRATION:
//   - API Gateway passes through Lambda's response directly
//   - Lambda MUST include CORS headers in EVERY response (not just OPTIONS)
//   - Add to all Lambda responses:
//     Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
//   - This is the most common mistake: OPTIONS works but actual requests fail
//     because Lambda's response doesn't include CORS headers
//
// FOR HTTP API:
//   - Simpler: configure CORS in one place (HTTP API CORS settings)
//   - API Gateway adds CORS headers automatically to all responses
//   - Lambda does NOT need to add CORS headers manually
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - CORS = browser-enforced; Postman/curl don't enforce it
// - Preflight OPTIONS request must be handled by API Gateway
// - Lambda proxy: Lambda must include CORS headers in responses
// - Non-proxy integration: API Gateway can add headers via response mapping
// - '*' for Access-Control-Allow-Origin: allows ALL origins (less secure)
// - Credentials (cookies, Authorization): cannot use '*', must specify exact origin

starterCode:
// Problem:
// Order service → (sync HTTP) → Fulfillment service
// Order rate: 5,000 orders/min (during flash sale)
// Fulfillment rate: 500 orders/min (processing limit)
// Result: 90% of orders dropped during peak
//
// Requirement:
// - No orders dropped during peak (buffer excess orders)
// - Failed orders after retries: alert engineering team, store for investigation
// - Fulfillment service processes at its own rate (no change to fulfillment code)
//
// Question: How does SQS solve this problem?
// How do you configure retries and DLQ?
// What are the SQS visibility timeout implications?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: SQS Standard Queue between Order Service and Fulfillment Service
//
// ARCHITECTURE:
// Order Service → SQS Queue (buffer) → Fulfillment Service (polls)
//                                     ↓ (after maxReceiveCount failures)
//                                    DLQ (Dead Letter Queue)
//                                     ↓
//                                    SNS/CloudWatch Alarm → Engineering Alert
//
// HOW SQS DECOUPLES THE SERVICES:
// - Order Service: send order to SQS → returns immediately (async)
//   No longer blocked waiting for fulfillment
//   Can accept 5,000 orders/min without dropping any
// - SQS Queue: buffers orders persistently (up to 14 days retention)
//   Orders wait in queue until fulfillment is ready
// - Fulfillment Service: polls SQS at its own pace (500 orders/min)
//   Processes messages independently, not overwhelmed
// - Result: orders arrive at 5,000/min, fulfillment processes at 500/min
//   Queue depth builds during flash sale, drains afterward
//
// SQS CONFIGURATION:
//   Message Retention: 4 days (default) to 14 days
//   Visibility Timeout: >= fulfillment processing time
//     If processing takes max 60 sec: set visibility timeout to 60+ sec
//     During processing: message is 'invisible' to other consumers
//     If processing fails/crashes: message reappears after visibility timeout
//
// DEAD LETTER QUEUE (DLQ) SETUP:
//   1. Create a separate SQS queue as DLQ
//   2. Configure redrive policy on main queue:
//      - Dead Letter Queue: arn of DLQ
//      - Maximum Receive Count: 3 (retry 3 times before DLQ)
//   3. After 3 failed processing attempts: message moves to DLQ automatically
//   4. Set CloudWatch Alarm on DLQ ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible > 0
//   5. Alarm triggers SNS → email to engineering team
//
// VISIBILITY TIMEOUT CRITICAL RULE:
//   Visibility Timeout must be > processing time × 1.5 (safety buffer)
//   Too short: message becomes visible during processing → double processing
//   Too long: failed messages take too long to retry
//   Formula: Visibility Timeout >= max processing time + safety margin
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - SQS: async decoupling, persistent buffering, absorbs traffic spikes
// - Visibility Timeout: hides message from other consumers during processing
// - DLQ: messages that fail maxReceiveCount times (not time-based)
// - SQS Standard: at-least-once delivery, best-effort ordering
// - SQS FIFO: exactly-once processing, strict ordering, lower throughput
// - Max retention: 14 days; max message size: 256 KB

starterCode:
// Workload 1: Financial transactions
// - Must process in EXACT order (deposit before withdrawal)
// - Must process EXACTLY ONCE (no duplicate processing)
// - Volume: 500 transactions/second
//
// Workload 2: Image thumbnail generation
// - Order doesn't matter (any image can be processed in any order)
// - High throughput: 50,000 images/second
// - Occasional duplicate processing is acceptable
//
// Question: When to use Standard vs FIFO queue?
// What is message deduplication in FIFO?
// What are the throughput limits of each?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// SQS STANDARD QUEUE:
//   Delivery: AT-LEAST-ONCE (may deliver same message more than once)
//   Ordering: BEST-EFFORT (approximately ordered, not guaranteed)
//   Throughput: UNLIMITED (effectively unlimited API calls per second)
//   Deduplication: NOT built-in (consumer must handle duplicates idempotently)
//   Use case: highest throughput, order-independent workloads
//   → Workload 2 (thumbnail generation): 50,000 img/sec, order irrelevant
//
// SQS FIFO QUEUE:
//   Delivery: EXACTLY-ONCE processing (deduplication built-in)
//   Ordering: STRICT FIFO within a message group
//   Throughput: 300 transactions/sec (without batching)
//              3,000 transactions/sec (with batching, up to 10 messages/batch)
//   Deduplication: via MessageDeduplicationId
//   MessageGroupId: groups messages for FIFO processing
//   → Workload 1 (financial): exactly-once, ordered — BUT 500 tx/sec exceeds limit!
//
// FIFO THROUGHPUT LIMITATION FIX:
//   500 tx/sec exceeds FIFO's 300 tx/sec non-batched limit
//   Solutions:
//   a) Use batching: batch 2 messages → effectively 600 tx/sec
//   b) Multiple FIFO queues with routing logic
//   c) Partition by account: MessageGroupId = accountId → parallel groups
//      Each group maintains FIFO order; different groups process in parallel
//      Increases effective throughput with preserved per-account ordering
//
// FIFO MESSAGE CONCEPTS:
// MessageGroupId:
//   - All messages with same group ID are processed in order
//   - Different group IDs can be processed in parallel
//   - For financial: MessageGroupId = userId (orders per user are FIFO)
//
// MessageDeduplicationId:
//   - Unique ID per message submission
//   - If same ID sent within 5-minute deduplication window: duplicate discarded
//   - Can use content-based deduplication (hash of message body)
//
// FIFO QUEUE NAME MUST END WITH .fifo:
//   e.g., transactions.fifo (not transactions)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Standard: unlimited throughput, at-least-once, best-effort order
// - FIFO: 300/3,000 TPS, exactly-once, strict order within group
// - FIFO requires MessageGroupId (ordering scope) and deduplication ID
// - Content-based deduplication: hash of message body as dedup ID automatically
// - FIFO queues end in .fifo suffix (required naming convention)
// - S3 event notifications: CANNOT use FIFO queues as target

starterCode:
// Payment processor workflow:
// 1. Read message from SQS (order: $50 charge)
// 2. Call payment API → takes 45 seconds to complete
// 3. Delete message from SQS after payment succeeds
//
// SQS visibility timeout: 30 seconds
// Result: some payments charged TWICE
//
// Question: Why is the payment being charged twice?
// What is the root cause in terms of visibility timeout?
// How do you fix this?
// How can you extend visibility timeout for long-running processing?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS:
//
// Timeline:
//   T=0s:   Consumer A reads payment message from SQS
//           Message becomes INVISIBLE (30-second timeout starts)
//   T=30s:  Visibility timeout EXPIRES (processing still at 45%)
//           Message becomes VISIBLE again in queue
//   T=31s:  Consumer B reads the SAME payment message
//           Consumer B starts processing → calls payment API
//   T=45s:  Consumer A finishes → calls payment API → charges customer #1
//           Consumer A deletes message from SQS
//   T=76s:  Consumer B finishes → calls payment API → charges customer #2 (DUPLICATE)
//           Consumer B tries to delete message but it was already deleted (no-op)
//
// RESULT: Customer charged TWICE because visibility timeout < processing time
//
// FIX 1: Increase Visibility Timeout
//   Set visibility timeout to > maximum expected processing time
//   Formula: visibility timeout >= (max processing time) × 1.5
//   Example: 45 sec × 1.5 = 68 sec minimum → set to 90 or 120 seconds
//
// FIX 2: Extend Visibility Timeout Programmatically
//   Use ChangeMessageVisibility API during processing:
//   - Extend timeout while processing is in progress
//   - Before current timeout expires: extend by another N seconds
//   - Allows indefinite processing with periodic heartbeat extensions
//   Example: every 20 seconds, extend visibility by 30 more seconds
//   Max visibility timeout: 12 hours
//
// FIX 3: Idempotency at the Payment API Level
//   - Include unique idempotency key in payment API call
//   - Payment provider deduplicates: second call with same key → no-op
//   - Defense in depth: even if double-read occurs, double-charge prevented
//
// CORRECT CONFIGURATION:
//   Visibility Timeout: 120 seconds (2x expected processing + buffer)
//   Max Receive Count: 3 (retry 3 times before DLQ)
//   DLQ retention: 14 days (investigate failed payments)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Visibility Timeout MUST be > processing time (or double processing occurs)
// - ChangeMessageVisibility: extend timeout mid-processing
// - Max visibility timeout: 12 hours
// - Default visibility timeout: 30 seconds
// - At-least-once delivery means idempotent consumers are essential for critical ops
// - Payment systems: always use idempotency keys as defense-in-depth

starterCode:
// When 'OrderPlaced' event fires, ALL of these must receive it:
// 1. Inventory Service: reserve stock (can be slow, owns SQS queue)
// 2. Shipping Service: create label (can be slow, owns SQS queue)
// 3. Notification Service: send email (can be slow, owns SQS queue)
// 4. Analytics Service: record event (can be slow, owns SQS queue)
//
// Requirements:
// - All four services receive EVERY order event
// - Each processes at its own rate without affecting others
// - Events not lost if a service is temporarily down
//
// Question: Why not send directly to 4 SQS queues from the Order Service?
// How does SNS + SQS fan-out solve this?
// What is topic-based vs point-to-point messaging?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SOLUTION: SNS Topic + SQS Queues (Fan-Out Pattern)
//
// ARCHITECTURE:
// Order Service → SNS Topic (OrderPlaced)
//                      ↓ fan-out (parallel, async)
//                 ┌─────┬─────┬─────┐
//                SQS   SQS   SQS   SQS
//               (Inv) (Ship)(Notif)(Ana)
//                 ↓     ↓     ↓     ↓
//              Inv   Ship  Notif  Analytics
//             Service Service Service Service
//
// WHY NOT SEND DIRECTLY TO 4 SQS QUEUES:
//   Tight coupling: Order Service must know all 4 queues
//   Adding 5th service: requires changing Order Service code
//   Ordering Service failure: if one queue write fails, partial delivery
//   Code complexity: 4 SDK calls instead of 1
//
// HOW SNS + SQS FAN-OUT WORKS:
//   1. Create SNS Topic: OrderPlacedTopic
//   2. Each service creates their own SQS queue
//   3. Each SQS queue subscribes to SNS Topic
//      (SNS needs permission to write to SQS: SQS access policy)
//   4. Order Service publishes ONE message to SNS Topic
//   5. SNS delivers a copy to ALL 4 SQS queues (fan-out)
//   6. Each service independently polls its own SQS queue
//
// BENEFITS:
//   - Loose coupling: Order Service knows only the SNS topic ARN
//   - Adding new service: subscribe new SQS to topic (no Order Service change)
//   - Durability: SQS persists messages for each service independently
//   - Service autonomy: Inventory slow? Only Inventory SQS backlog grows
//   - Failure isolation: Shipping down? Other services unaffected
//
// SQS ACCESS POLICY FOR SNS SUBSCRIPTION:
//   Allow sns:SendMessage from SNS Topic ARN to each SQS queue
//   (Required for cross-account or explicit permission)
//
// FIFO CONSIDERATION:
//   SNS FIFO Topic → SQS FIFO Queue: maintains ordering for fan-out
//   Standard SNS Topic → Standard SQS: best-effort ordering
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Fan-out: SNS (1 topic) → multiple SQS queues (each service gets own copy)
// - SNS = pub/sub (point of decoupling); SQS = durable queue per consumer
// - Adding subscriber: no publisher changes needed
// - SQS must have access policy allowing SNS to publish
// - SNS FIFO + SQS FIFO: maintains ordering in fan-out scenarios

starterCode:
// SNS Topic: OrderEvents
// Subscribers:
// 1. FraudDetectionQueue: needs orders where amount > $500
// 2. PriorityFulfillmentQueue: needs orders where priority = 'URGENT'
// 3. GeneralFulfillmentQueue: needs all orders where priority != 'URGENT'
//
// Currently: all 3 queues receive ALL orders (no filtering)
// Problem: fraud service processes unnecessary low-value orders
//
// Question: How do SNS message filter policies work?
// Where are filter policies configured?
// Can you filter on message attributes OR on the message body?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// SNS MESSAGE FILTERING:
//   - Filter policies applied PER SUBSCRIPTION (not on the topic)
//   - Publisher sends ONE message to topic with message attributes
//   - SNS evaluates each subscriber's filter policy
//   - Message delivered only to subscribers whose filter matches
//   - No change needed in publisher code (add attributes, same SendMessage call)
//
// PUBLISHER MUST INCLUDE MESSAGE ATTRIBUTES:
//   SNS.publish(
//     TopicArn: 'arn:...:OrderEvents',
//     Message: '{"orderId": "O001", "amount": 750, ...}',
//     MessageAttributes: {
//       'amount': {DataType: 'Number', Value: '750'},
//       'priority': {DataType: 'String', Value: 'URGENT'}
//     }
//   )
//
// FILTER POLICY 1 (FraudDetectionQueue):
// {
//   "amount": [{"numeric": [">", 500]}]
// }
// → Receives: orders where amount > 500
// → Skips: orders where amount <= 500
//
// FILTER POLICY 2 (PriorityFulfillmentQueue):
// {
//   "priority": ["URGENT"]
// }
// → Receives: orders where priority = 'URGENT'
//
// FILTER POLICY 3 (GeneralFulfillmentQueue):
// {
//   "priority": [{"anything-but": "URGENT"}]
// }
// → Receives: orders where priority is anything except 'URGENT'
// → Including orders with NO priority attribute
//
// FILTER POLICY OPERATORS:
//   String: exact match ["URGENT"], prefix [{"prefix": "ORD"}]
//   Numeric: [{"numeric": [">", 500]}], [{"numeric": ["=", 100]}]
//   Exists: [{"exists": true}] (attribute must be present)
//   Anything-but: [{"anything-but": "CANCELLED"}]
//   AND logic: multiple attribute conditions in same policy = AND
//   OR logic: multiple values in same attribute array = OR
//
// MESSAGE BODY FILTERING (newer feature):
//   - Filter on fields within the JSON message body (not just attributes)
//   - Enable on subscription: FilterPolicyScope = MessageBody
//   - Same operators as attribute filtering
//   - Avoids requiring publisher to add message attributes
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Filter policy on SUBSCRIPTION (per-subscriber, not per-topic)
// - Publisher must include message attributes for attribute-based filtering
// - OR logic: multiple values in same attribute condition
// - AND logic: multiple separate attribute conditions
// - No filter policy = receives ALL messages (default behavior)
// - Messages NOT matching any subscriber filter: not delivered (not sent to DLQ)

starterCode:
// Consumer polls SQS every 1 second
// Messages arrive: ~1 per minute (60-second average interval)
// Cost problem: 95% of polls return empty (billed for each API call)
// API calls: 60 per minute, but only 1 actually has a message
//
// Question: What is the difference between short polling and long polling?
// How does long polling reduce SQS API costs?
// What is the maximum long polling wait time?
// How do you enable long polling?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// SHORT POLLING (default, ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds = 0):
//   - SQS queries a SUBSET of its servers immediately
//   - Returns immediately: with messages if available, OR empty if no messages
//   - Each empty response costs the same as a response with messages
//   - 60 API calls/min × 59 empty = 59 wasted, billed API calls
//   - High CPU/network usage for polling application
//   - Use case: when low latency (instant delivery) is critical and cost is secondary
//
// LONG POLLING (ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds = 1-20 seconds):
//   - SQS queries ALL servers and waits up to 20 seconds for messages to arrive
//   - Returns as soon as messages are available (or after wait time if still empty)
//   - Reduces empty responses dramatically
//   - Result: ~1 API call/min (returns when message arrives) vs 60 calls/min
//   - Cost reduction: ~98% fewer API calls in this scenario
//   - Latency: message is delivered within (0-1 seconds typically) — not meaningful delay
//   - Maximum wait time: 20 seconds per ReceiveMessage call
//
// HOW TO ENABLE LONG POLLING:
// Option 1: Queue level (applies to all consumers)
//   - Set ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds on the SQS queue: 1-20 seconds
//   - All ReceiveMessage calls use this wait time automatically
//
// Option 2: Per API call (consumer-side)
//   - Pass WaitTimeSeconds parameter in each ReceiveMessage call
//   - Overrides queue-level setting for that specific call
//
// RECOMMENDATION FOR THIS SCENARIO:
//   Set ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds = 20 on the queue
//   Consumer polls once: SQS waits up to 20 sec for messages
//   Message arrives → returned immediately (<20 sec wait)
//   No message in 20 sec → returns empty, consumer calls again
//   Result: max 3 empty API calls/min instead of 60
//
// COST COMPARISON:
//   Short polling: 60 API calls/min = 3,600/hr = 86,400/day
//   Long polling (20s wait): ~3 API calls/min = 180/hr = 4,320/day
//   Cost reduction: ~95%
//   Price per million API calls: $0.40 → significant savings at scale
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Default is short polling (WaitTimeSeconds = 0)
// - Long polling reduces empty responses and costs (NOT slower for delivered messages)
// - Max wait time: 20 seconds
// - Enable on queue (ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds) or per-call (WaitTimeSeconds)
// - Long polling is almost always preferred over short polling
// - Lambda event source mapping uses long polling automatically

starterCode:
// Two RDS problems to solve:
// 1. Occasional primary DB failures cause 5-20 minutes of downtime
// 2. Analytics queries slow down the OLTP workload on the same DB
//
// Two RDS features to choose from:
// - Multi-AZ Deployment
// - Read Replicas
//
// Question: What does each feature do? Which solves which problem?
// How does failover work in Multi-AZ?
// Can Read Replicas serve strongly consistent reads?
// Can you use both features together?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// RDS MULTI-AZ DEPLOYMENT:
//   Purpose: HIGH AVAILABILITY (HA), not performance
//   How it works:
//   - RDS maintains a synchronous STANDBY replica in a DIFFERENT AZ
//   - All writes to primary are synchronously replicated to standby
//   - Standby is NOT accessible for reads (it's just a hot standby)
//   - Automatic failover: if primary fails, RDS updates DNS endpoint to standby
//   - Failover time: 60-120 seconds (DNS TTL + instance promotion)
//   - No manual intervention required for failover
//   - Replication: SYNCHRONOUS (zero data loss on failover)
//   → SOLVES: Problem 1 (downtime from DB failures)
//
// RDS READ REPLICAS:
//   Purpose: READ SCALABILITY and ANALYTICS OFFLOADING
//   How it works:
//   - Asynchronous replication from primary to one or more Read Replicas
//   - Read Replicas: separate RDS instances that can serve SELECT queries
//   - Point analytics applications to Read Replica endpoint (separate DNS)
//   - Frees primary for write-heavy OLTP workload
//   - Replication: ASYNCHRONOUS (possible lag, eventual consistency)
//   - Data may be slightly behind primary (replication lag)
//   - Can be in same AZ, different AZ, or different REGION (CRR)
//   - Cost: charged per Read Replica hour (same as primary)
//   → SOLVES: Problem 2 (analytics slowing OLTP)
//
// CAN YOU USE BOTH?
//   YES — Multi-AZ + Read Replicas can be used simultaneously:
//   - Multi-AZ standby provides failover protection
//   - Read Replicas serve analytics traffic
//   - Read Replica can also be Multi-AZ (standby within read replica)
//
// KEY DIFFERENCES:
//   Feature          | Multi-AZ          | Read Replicas
//   Replication      | Synchronous       | Asynchronous
//   Standby readable | NO                | YES
//   Failover         | Automatic (DNS)   | Manual (promote to primary)
//   Data loss risk   | Zero (sync)       | Possible (async lag)
//   Purpose          | HA/Failover       | Read scaling
//   Region support   | Same region only  | Cross-region possible
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Multi-AZ = HA, sync replication, standby NOT readable, auto failover
// - Read Replicas = performance, async replication, replicas ARE readable
// - Multi-AZ standby: only accessible after promotion (failover)
// - Read Replica promotion: manual, used for disaster recovery
// - Up to 15 Read Replicas per RDS MySQL/PostgreSQL instance
// - Cross-region Read Replicas: disaster recovery + global read latency

starterCode:
// RDS MySQL problem:
// - CPU: 80% (high)
// - Read latency: 2-3 seconds (unacceptable)
// - Connections: 480/500 (near limit)
// - Workload: 90% reads, 10% writes
// - Reads: mostly same articles over and over (hot content)
//
// Question: What are three different strategies to solve this?
// What type of data is best suited for ElastiCache vs Read Replicas?
// How does RDS Proxy help with the connection limit problem?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// THREE COMPLEMENTARY STRATEGIES:
//
// STRATEGY 1: ElastiCache (for hot/cacheable reads)
//   Problem solved: high CPU, high read latency for frequently-accessed content
//   How: Application checks cache first; if miss, query RDS and cache result
//   Pattern: Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading)
//     1. Check cache for article-123
//     2. Cache HIT: return from cache (microseconds, no DB call)
//     3. Cache MISS: query RDS, store in cache with TTL, return result
//   TTL: balance freshness vs cache benefit (e.g., 5-15 minutes for article content)
//   Best for: data that:
//     - Is read frequently (high read-to-write ratio)
//     - Can tolerate slight staleness (TTL-based invalidation)
//     - Is expensive to compute or query
//   Result: 90% cache hit rate → 90% fewer RDS queries → CPU drops significantly
//
// STRATEGY 2: Read Replicas (for complex queries not suitable for caching)
//   Problem solved: remaining DB CPU for analytics/complex queries
//   How: route read-heavy analytics to Read Replica endpoint
//   Best for: complex queries, reporting, queries returning large datasets
//   Read Replica is in different AZ — load-balance reads across primary + replicas
//   Note: Read Replicas help with CPU but not connection limit
//
// STRATEGY 3: RDS Proxy (for connection limit issue)
//   Problem solved: 480/500 connections at limit
//   How: Application connects to Proxy (short-lived connections OK)
//         Proxy maintains connection POOL to RDS (fewer, longer-lived connections)
//   Result: 1,000+ app connections → Proxy → 50 pooled RDS connections
//   Also: faster failover (Multi-AZ), IAM authentication
//   Best for: serverless (Lambda) or microservices with many connection sources
//
// COMBINED IMPACT:
//   ElastiCache: reduces RDS read queries by 90%
//   Read Replicas: distributes remaining complex reads
//   RDS Proxy: solves connection exhaustion
//   Together: CPU from 80% to <20%, latency from 2-3s to <100ms, connections OK
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - ElastiCache: best for hot, cacheable data (high read/write ratio)
// - Read Replicas: best for reads that can't be cached (complex, unique queries)
// - RDS Proxy: best for connection limit issues and Lambda → RDS patterns
// - Cache-Aside: check cache first, load from DB on miss, populate cache
// - Write-Through: update cache when writing to DB (always fresh, more complex)

starterCode:
// Use Case 1: User session cache
// - Must survive a single node failure (data must persist across failures)
// - Multi-AZ needed
// - Session data uses complex data structures (sorted sets for leaderboard)
//
// Use Case 2: Product catalog cache
// - Simple object caching (strings)
// - Need to scale horizontally to many nodes
// - OK to lose cache on node failure (warm from DB)
// - Prefer simplest possible configuration
//
// Question: What are the key architectural differences between Redis and Memcached?
// Which is appropriate for each use case?
// What data structures does Redis support that Memcached does not?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// REDIS:
//   Architecture: single-threaded (one core), master/replica replication
//   Persistence: YES — RDB snapshots + AOF (append-only file)
//   Replication: YES — read replicas, automatic failover (Redis Sentinel in ElastiCache)
//   Multi-AZ: YES — primary + replica in different AZs
//   Data Structures: strings, hashes, lists, sets, SORTED SETS, bitmaps, HyperLogLog
//   Pub/Sub: YES
//   Lua scripting: YES
//   Transactions: YES (MULTI/EXEC)
//   Max item size: 512 MB
//   Use case: sessions, leaderboards, pub/sub, rate limiting, complex data structures
//   → Use for: Use Case 1 (sessions with persistence + Multi-AZ + sorted sets)
//
// MEMCACHED:
//   Architecture: multi-threaded (multiple cores), no replication
//   Persistence: NO — all data lost on node restart
//   Replication: NO — each node is independent
//   Multi-AZ: Sort of (cluster spans AZs, but no node-level failover)
//   Data Structures: strings only (simple key-value)
//   Simple horizontal scaling: add/remove nodes easily (consistent hashing)
//   Max item size: 1 MB
//   Use case: simple object caching, session stores (if loss is OK), page fragment caching
//   → Use for: Use Case 2 (simple product catalog, OK to lose cache)
//
// COMPARISON TABLE:
//   Feature          | Redis        | Memcached
//   Persistence      | YES (RDB/AOF)| NO
//   Replication      | YES          | NO
//   Multi-AZ failover| YES          | NO
//   Data Structures  | Rich (6+)    | String only
//   Multi-threading  | NO (1 thread)| YES (multi-core)
//   Pub/Sub          | YES          | NO
//   Horizontal scale | Via sharding | Native multi-node
//   Max object size  | 512 MB       | 1 MB
//
// REDIS DATA STRUCTURES USE CASES:
//   String:       Simple cache values, counters
//   Hash:         User profile objects, product details
//   List:         Recent items, activity feeds
//   Set:          Unique items, tags
//   Sorted Set:   Leaderboards, ranked data (score-based ordering)
//   HyperLogLog:  Approximate unique visitor counting
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Redis: rich features, persistence, replication, use for production session stores
// - Memcached: simple, fast, multi-threaded, use for pure ephemeral caching
// - ElastiCache for Redis: supports Redis AUTH, TLS, encryption at rest
// - Exam default answer: Redis (more features, more exam coverage)
// - Memcached: no persistence or replication = simplest, but ephemeral

starterCode:
// RDS backup requirements:
// 1. Recover to any point-in-time within last 7 days (transaction-level recovery)
// 2. Retain some backups for 90 days (compliance)
// 3. Copy backups to a second region (disaster recovery)
// 4. Test recovery procedure without affecting production
//
// Question: What is the difference between automated backups and manual snapshots?
// What is Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR) and what does it require?
// How do you enable cross-region backup copies?
// What happens to automated backups when the RDS instance is deleted?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// AUTOMATED BACKUPS:
//   - Enabled by default, retention: 0-35 days (0 = disabled)
//   - Daily full backup during backup window (configurable off-peak time)
//   - Continuous transaction log backups (every 5 minutes)
//   - ENABLES Point-in-Time Recovery: restore to ANY second within retention period
//   - Storage: stored in S3, charged per GB-month beyond provisioned storage
//   - DELETED when: RDS instance is deleted (unless you request a final snapshot)
//   - DELETED when: retention period expires
//   - Cannot copy automated backups directly to another region
//     (but automated backups can be used for PITR within the source region)
//
// MANUAL SNAPSHOTS:
//   - User-initiated (or automated via Lambda/AWS Backup)
//   - Retention: FOREVER (persist until explicitly deleted)
//   - Point-in-time: snapshots capture the DB state at the moment of the snapshot
//     NOT continuous — only restore to the snapshot moment (not between snapshots)
//   - Can be COPIED to another region (cross-region DR)
//   - Can be SHARED with other AWS accounts
//   - Storage: S3, charged per GB-month
//
// POINT-IN-TIME RECOVERY (PITR):
//   - Requires automated backups with retention > 0
//   - Combines: daily backup + transaction logs between backups
//   - Can restore to any second from 5 minutes ago up to retention period end
//   - PITR creates a NEW RDS instance (does not restore in-place)
//   - Recovery time: depends on size and time of restore point
//
// SOLVING THE REQUIREMENTS:
//
// Req 1 (PITR within 7 days):
//   → Enable automated backups with retention = 7 days
//   → Transaction logs enable restore to any second
//
// Req 2 (90-day compliance retention):
//   → Manual snapshots every day/week (automated via AWS Backup or Lambda)
//   → Manual snapshots persist for 90 days (then delete manually or via rule)
//   → OR: use AWS Backup to manage retention policy automatically
//
// Req 3 (Cross-region DR):
//   → Copy manual snapshots to DR region (manually or via AWS Backup)
//   → Enable automated backup replication (newer RDS feature)
//
// Req 4 (Test recovery without affecting prod):
//   → Restore snapshot to a NEW RDS instance
//   → PITR always creates a NEW instance (non-destructive)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Automated backups: PITR enabled, deleted when instance deleted or period expires
// - Manual snapshots: persist forever, cross-region copyable, snapshot-point-only restore
// - PITR = new instance, not in-place restore
// - Deleting RDS instance: automated backups deleted; create final snapshot!
// - AWS Backup: centralized backup management, compliance, cross-region copy automation

starterCode:
// Lambda + RDS problem:
// 500 concurrent Lambda executions, each opening a new DB connection
// RDS db.r5.large: ~600 max connections
// Error: 'remaining connection slots reserved' at ~580 connections
//
// Each Lambda:
// - Opens connection on initialization
// - Keeps connection in global scope (warm Lambda reuse)
// - But 500 concurrent Lambdas = 500 simultaneous connections
//
// Question: Why does Lambda exhaust the RDS connection pool?
// What is RDS Proxy and how does it solve this?
// What other benefits does RDS Proxy provide?
// What is the cost model for RDS Proxy?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// WHY LAMBDA EXHAUSTS RDS CONNECTIONS:
//   Traditional apps: 1 connection pool per app process, shared by threads
//   Lambda: each EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT has its own connection
//   500 concurrent Lambdas = 500 separate execution environments
//   Each environment opens 1 connection = 500 simultaneous connections
//   With scaling: 1,000-3,000 concurrent Lambdas = connection storm
//   Even with connection in global scope (warm reuse), scale-out still creates new connections
//
// SOLUTION: RDS PROXY
//   Architecture:
//   Lambda (500+ concurrent) → RDS Proxy (connection pool) → RDS (50-100 connections)
//
//   How it works:
//   - Lambda connects to Proxy endpoint (fast, lightweight)
//   - Proxy maintains long-lived connection pool to RDS
//   - Multiplexes MANY Lambda connections into FEW RDS connections
//   - Connections are shared: when Lambda's SQL is not executing, connection is freed
//   - Result: 500 Lambda executions → ~50-100 actual RDS connections
//
// RDS PROXY ADDITIONAL BENEFITS:
//
// 1. Faster failover (Multi-AZ):
//    - Standard RDS failover: 20-60 seconds (DNS propagation)
//    - RDS Proxy failover: <5 seconds (proxy routes to new primary seamlessly)
//    - Application connections to Proxy persist across RDS failover
//
// 2. IAM Authentication:
//    - Lambda authenticates to Proxy using IAM token (no password storage)
//    - Proxy authenticates to RDS using Secrets Manager credentials
//    - Zero credential management in Lambda code
//
// 3. Secrets Manager Integration:
//    - Proxy fetches DB credentials from Secrets Manager automatically
//    - Supports automatic credential rotation without app restarts
//
// CONFIGURATION:
//   - Create RDS Proxy → associate with RDS instance
//   - Set target DB connection pool size (e.g., 100 connections to RDS)
//   - Lambda connects to Proxy endpoint (instead of RDS endpoint)
//   - Lambda execution role needs: rds-db:connect permission
//
// COST:
//   - ~$0.015 per vCPU per hour of associated RDS instance
//   - For db.r5.large (2 vCPU): ~$0.03/hour, ~$22/month
//   - No per-connection charge
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - RDS Proxy: connection pooler for serverless/microservices → RDS
// - Reduces DB connections from N Lambda executors to a small pool
// - Faster Multi-AZ failover (< 5 seconds vs 20-60 seconds without Proxy)
// - Supports IAM authentication (no passwords in Lambda code)
// - Supports Secrets Manager for automatic credential rotation
// - Must be in same VPC as RDS (no cross-VPC without PrivateLink)

starterCode:
// Private subnet EC2 instances need OUTBOUND internet access.
// Requirement: instances must NOT be reachable from the internet inbound.
// Team size: small, cannot manage complex infrastructure.
//
// Options:
// A) NAT Gateway (AWS managed)
// B) NAT Instance (EC2 instance running NAT software)
//
// Question: What is the difference between NAT Gateway and NAT Instance?
// Which is best for 'least operational overhead'?
// Where must the NAT Gateway/Instance be placed?
// What route table change is needed?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// COMPARISON:
//
// NAT GATEWAY (AWS Managed Service):
//   Availability: Highly available within AZ (AWS-managed redundancy)
//   Scaling: Automatic (up to 45 Gbps)
//   Patching: AWS handles all software updates
//   Configuration: Create, assign Elastic IP, update route table
//   Security Groups: NOT applicable (can't assign SG to NAT Gateway)
//   Bandwidth: 5 Gbps → scales to 45 Gbps
//   Cost: ~$0.045/hr + $0.045/GB processed
//   Failure mode: AZ failure → NAT in that AZ fails (create one per AZ for HA)
//   Best for: production workloads requiring LEAST OPERATIONAL OVERHEAD
//   → RECOMMENDED ANSWER
//
// NAT INSTANCE (Self-Managed EC2):
//   Availability: Must configure manually (Auto Scaling + EIP failover scripts)
//   Scaling: Manual (change instance type)
//   Patching: You must patch the AMI and manage the EC2 instance
//   Configuration: Launch EC2, disable Source/Dest Check, configure iptables
//   Security Groups: YES (applied to the EC2 instance)
//   Bandwidth: Limited by instance type (t3.micro has low bandwidth)
//   Cost: EC2 instance cost (can be cheaper for low traffic with t3.micro)
//   Best for: cost-sensitive scenarios, custom routing requirements
//   NAT Instance setup requires: disable Source/Destination Check on the EC2
//
// REQUIRED CONFIGURATION (same for both):
// 1. Place NAT Gateway/Instance in PUBLIC SUBNET
//    (public subnet = has route to Internet Gateway)
// 2. Assign Elastic IP to NAT Gateway/Instance
// 3. Update PRIVATE SUBNET route table:
//    Destination: 0.0.0.0/0
//    Target: nat-gateway-ID  (or NAT Instance ID)
//
// HIGH AVAILABILITY DESIGN:
//   Create NAT Gateway in EACH AZ separately:
//   - AZ-A private subnet → route to NAT Gateway in AZ-A public subnet
//   - AZ-B private subnet → route to NAT Gateway in AZ-B public subnet
//   - If one AZ fails, other AZ's private subnet still has NAT
//   - Cost: 2x (one NAT Gateway per AZ)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - NAT Gateway: managed, no SG, automatic scaling, ~$0.045/hr, least ops overhead
// - NAT Instance: EC2, has SG, manual scaling/HA, requires Source/Dest Check disabled
// - Both: must be in PUBLIC subnet; private subnet routes to NAT
// - 'Least operational overhead' question = NAT Gateway is correct answer
// - NAT Gateway is NOT free tier eligible

starterCode:
// Three VPC connectivity scenarios:
// 1. One-to-many: VPC A (monitoring) needs to be reachable from 14 other VPCs
// 2. Full mesh: 15 VPCs need any-to-any communication
// 3. Selective access: third-party vendor needs access to ONLY one API endpoint
//
// Question: When should you use VPC Peering vs Transit Gateway vs PrivateLink?
// What is the transitive routing limitation of VPC Peering?
// What are the cost differences?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// VPC PEERING:
//   What: direct network connection between exactly 2 VPCs (any region, any account)
//   Routing: each VPC needs routes pointing to peered VPC's CIDR
//   Transitive: NO — if A peers with B and B peers with C, A cannot talk to C via B
//   Use case: simple 2-VPC connectivity, low VPC count scenarios
//   Cost: free to create; data transfer charges apply
//   For 15 VPCs full mesh: n(n-1)/2 = 105 peering connections + route table maintenance
//   → NOT practical for Scenario 2 (full mesh of 15 VPCs)
//
// TRANSIT GATEWAY:
//   What: regional hub that VPCs and VPNs connect to (hub-and-spoke model)
//   Routing: VPCs connect to TGW; TGW routes between all attached VPCs
//   Transitive: YES — VPCs communicate via TGW (one attachment per VPC)
//   Use case: connecting many VPCs, VPN attachments, Direct Connect
//   Cost: per attachment ($0.05/hr) + per GB data transferred
//   For 15 VPCs: 15 attachments instead of 105 peering connections
//   → BEST for Scenario 2 (full mesh), also for Scenario 1 if bidirectional needed
//
// VPC PRIVATELINK:
//   What: exposes a specific SERVICE endpoint (NLB) from one VPC to another
//   Routing: consumers access only the specific endpoint, NOT the VPC network
//   Transitive: N/A — not full network connectivity, only specific service endpoint
//   Use case: share a service with many consumers without VPC-level access
//   Cost: per Interface Endpoint hour + per GB
//   For Scenario 1 (monitoring service): expose only the monitoring API, not entire VPC
//   For Scenario 3 (third-party access): expose only the API, vendor has no VPC access
//   → BEST for Scenario 1 and Scenario 3 (selective service access)
//
// SOLUTION MAPPING:
// Scenario 1 (share monitoring VPC with 14 others):
//   → PrivateLink: monitoring VPC creates NLB, exposes via endpoint service
//     Other VPCs create Interface Endpoints pointing to endpoint service
//     Consumers access monitoring service without full VPC access
//
// Scenario 2 (15-VPC full mesh):
//   → Transit Gateway: each VPC attaches to TGW, any-to-any routing
//
// Scenario 3 (third-party vendor accesses one API):
//   → PrivateLink: expose API endpoint via Interface Endpoint
//     Vendor creates Interface Endpoint in their VPC
//     No access to your VPC network beyond that specific endpoint
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Peering: 1-to-1, no transitive routing, free connections
// - Transit Gateway: hub-and-spoke, transitive, manageable at scale (15+ VPCs)
// - PrivateLink: service-level access only, most secure, cross-account & third-party
// - Peering CIDR must not overlap (true for all VPC connectivity options)
// - TGW supports inter-region peering between Transit Gateways

starterCode:
// Workload 1: Microservices API
// - Route /users to User Service ECS tasks
// - Route /orders to Order Service ECS tasks
// - Route based on HTTP headers (e.g., X-Tenant-ID header)
// - Need: authentication, WAF, HTTPS termination
//
// Workload 2: Financial trading system
// - Ultra-low latency (<1ms)
// - Static IP (partners whitelist by IP)
// - TCP/UDP connections at millions/sec
// - TLS passthrough (terminate at backend, not LB)
//
// Question: ALB vs NLB — which for each workload?
// What is the OSI layer each operates at?
// What target types do each support?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// APPLICATION LOAD BALANCER (ALB) — Layer 7:
//   OSI Layer: 7 (Application — HTTP/HTTPS/WebSocket)
//   Routing decisions: path, host, headers, query strings, HTTP method
//   SSL/TLS termination: YES (terminates at ALB, backend gets HTTP)
//   IP addresses: DNS-based, dynamic IPs (no static IPs)
//   WAF integration: YES (AWS WAF can be attached)
//   Authentication: YES (Cognito, OIDC via ALB listener rules)
//   Target types: EC2 instances, ECS tasks, IP addresses, Lambda functions
//   Use cases: microservices, content-based routing, HTTP APIs
//   Cost: charged per LCU (Load Capacity Unit) based on connections, requests, rules
//   → BEST for Workload 1 (microservices with path/header routing)
//
// NETWORK LOAD BALANCER (NLB) — Layer 4:
//   OSI Layer: 4 (Transport — TCP/UDP/TLS)
//   Routing decisions: IP + port (no HTTP content awareness)
//   SSL/TLS: passthrough (can pass TLS to backend) OR termination at NLB
//   IP addresses: STATIC (one Elastic IP per AZ, assignable)
//   WAF integration: NO
//   Authentication: NO
//   Target types: EC2 instances, IP addresses, ALB (NLB in front of ALB)
//   Performance: millions of requests/sec, ~100 microsecond latency
//   Use cases: gaming, IoT, financial trading, TCP/UDP applications
//   → BEST for Workload 2 (trading, static IP, low latency, TCP/UDP)
//
// ALB ROUTING RULES FOR WORKLOAD 1:
//   Listener Rule 1: IF path starts with /users → forward to UserService TargetGroup
//   Listener Rule 2: IF path starts with /orders → forward to OrderService TargetGroup
//   Listener Rule 3: IF header X-Tenant-ID = enterprise → forward to PremiumTargetGroup
//   Default: forward to DefaultTargetGroup (or return 404)
//
// NLB FOR WORKLOAD 2:
//   Assign Elastic IPs to NLB in each AZ → give static IPs to partners for whitelisting
//   TLS passthrough: NLB forwards encrypted traffic to backend (no termination)
//   UDP listener for data feed (NLB supports UDP; ALB does not)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - ALB: Layer 7, content-based routing, WAF, Cognito auth, no static IP
// - NLB: Layer 4, TCP/UDP, static Elastic IP per AZ, ultra-low latency, no WAF
// - ALB target types include Lambda (NLB does not support Lambda)
// - NLB supports TCP passthrough TLS (ALB always terminates TLS)
// - Both: support multiple AZ, health checks, cross-zone load balancing

starterCode:
// Canary deployment strategy:
// Phase 1: 5% to new version, 95% to old version
// Phase 2: 25% to new version, 75% to old version
// Phase 3: 50%/50%
// Phase 4: 100% to new version
// Rollback: instant 100% back to old version
//
// Two ALBs: alb-v1.region.elb.amazonaws.com and alb-v2.region.elb.amazonaws.com
//
// Question: Which Route53 routing policy enables this canary deployment?
// How does weighted routing work?
// How do you implement instant rollback?
// What are other Route53 routing policies and when are they used?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// ROUTING POLICY: Route53 Weighted Routing
//
// HOW WEIGHTED ROUTING WORKS:
//   - Assign numeric weights to DNS records for the same name
//   - Route53 routes traffic proportionally based on weights
//   - Weights are RELATIVE (not percentages), but using 0-100 is intuitive
//   - Traffic share = weight / sum of all weights
//
// IMPLEMENTATION:
// Both records for api.example.com:
//   Record 1: CNAME to alb-v1, Weight: 95
//   Record 2: CNAME to alb-v2, Weight: 5
//   → 5% goes to alb-v2, 95% goes to alb-v1
//
// PHASE PROGRESSION (update weights in console or via API):
//   Phase 1: v1=95, v2=5   → 5% to new version
//   Phase 2: v1=75, v2=25  → 25% to new version
//   Phase 3: v1=50, v2=50  → 50/50 split
//   Phase 4: v1=0, v2=100  → 100% to new version
//
// INSTANT ROLLBACK:
//   Set v2 weight = 0 (exclude from routing) → 100% back to v1
//   Weight = 0: record is NEVER served (effectively removed)
//   Weight = 0 keeps the record in place for quick re-enable
//   Caveat: TTL delay — DNS changes take TTL seconds to propagate
//   Low TTL (60 seconds): faster rollback (but higher DNS query costs)
//
// OTHER ROUTE53 ROUTING POLICIES:
//
// Simple: one record, one destination (default)
// Weighted: % split between multiple destinations
// Latency-based: route to lowest-latency region automatically
// Geolocation: route based on user's geographic location
// Geoproximity: route based on geographic proximity with bias
// Failover: primary/secondary — health check driven automatic failover
// Multi-value answer: return multiple IPs, client picks one (basic load balancing)
// IP-based: route based on client IP CIDR (for internal vs external traffic)
//
// USE CASES FOR EACH:
// Weighted: canary deployment, A/B testing, gradual rollouts
// Latency: multi-region apps serving global users
// Geolocation: compliance (data sovereignty), language-specific content
// Failover: active-passive DR (primary fails → health check → route to secondary)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Weighted routing: relative weights, update anytime, weight=0 excludes record
// - Health checks: can be associated with weighted records — unhealthy = not served
// - Low TTL for canary: faster traffic shift response, higher Route53 query costs
// - Weighted routing distributes at DNS level — not as precise as ALB weighted routing
// - For more precise traffic shifting: ALB weighted target groups (within one region)

starterCode:
// CloudFront distribution serving:
// 1. Static assets from S3: /static/* path, changes weekly
// 2. Dynamic API from API GW: /api/* path, personalized, changes frequently
//
// Goals:
// - Static: cache aggressively (long TTL, high cache hit rate)
// - Dynamic: cache appropriately (short TTL or no cache for personalized)
// - Both: global distribution, DDoS protection
//
// Question: How do you configure different caching behaviors per path?
// How do you serve both static and dynamic from one domain?
// What is cache invalidation and when is it needed?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// CLOUDFRONT DISTRIBUTION WITH MULTIPLE CACHE BEHAVIORS:
//
// BEHAVIOR 1: Static Assets (path pattern: /static/*)
//   Origin: S3 Bucket (use Origin Access Control — OAC)
//   Cache TTL:
//     Default TTL: 604800 seconds (7 days = 1 week)
//     Max TTL: 31536000 seconds (1 year)
//     Min TTL: 0
//   Cache Policy: CachingOptimized (AWS managed) OR custom
//   Viewer Protocol: Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
//   Origin Request Policy: no cookies, no query strings forwarded
//   Result: files cached at CloudFront edge for 7 days
//   On Deploy: CloudFront invalidation on /static/* OR versioned filenames
//
// BEHAVIOR 2: Dynamic API (path pattern: /api/*)
//   Origin: API Gateway custom domain
//   Cache TTL: based on sensitivity
//     Non-personalized (product list): TTL = 60 seconds (1 minute)
//     Personalized (user data): TTL = 0 (no caching) OR use Authorization header in cache key
//   Origin Request Policy: Forward all headers, cookies, query strings needed by API
//   If you include Authorization header in cache key:
//     Each user's response is cached separately (correct personalization)
//     But cache hit rate is low (one entry per user per request variant)
//   For truly personalized: TTL=0 (CloudFront passes all requests to origin)
//
// SERVING BOTH FROM ONE DOMAIN (example.com):
//   Single CloudFront distribution, two origins:
//   - S3 bucket origin for /static/*
//   - API Gateway origin for /api/*
//   Default behavior (/*): S3 origin (catch-all for root/HTML pages)
//   Priority: more specific patterns (/api/*, /static/*) take priority over /*
//
// CLOUDFRONT INVALIDATION:
//   When: static files change before TTL expires (emergency patch deployment)
//   Cost: first 1,000 paths/month free, then $0.005 per invalidation path
//   Best practice: versioned filenames (app-v2.3.js) → old cached files expire naturally
//     No invalidation needed: old files expire when TTL expires
//     Users get new version as their cache misses on new filename
//
// ORIGIN ACCESS CONTROL (OAC) for S3:
//   - CloudFront accesses S3 on behalf of viewer
//   - S3 bucket policy: only allow CloudFront service principal
//   - Prevents direct S3 URL access (forces traffic through CloudFront)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Cache behaviors: path-pattern based, separate TTL/policy per behavior
// - Static: long TTL + versioned filenames (avoid invalidation cost)
// - Dynamic personalized: TTL=0 OR include Authorization in cache key
// - OAC: restrict S3 access to CloudFront only
// - Invalidation: for emergency updates; versioned filenames are better practice

starterCode:
// CloudFormation stack update:
// Step 1: Modify RDS instance type (db.t3.micro → db.t3.small) — SUCCEEDS
// Step 2: Create new Lambda function with configuration — FAILS
//
// Question: What happens to the RDS instance change after Lambda fails?
// What states does the stack go through?
// How do you recover from UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED state?
// What is the difference between creating a stack and updating one?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// CLOUDFORMATION UPDATE FAILURE BEHAVIOR:
//
// STACK STATE PROGRESSION:
// 1. UPDATE_IN_PROGRESS
//    RDS type change applied (succeeds)
//    Lambda creation attempted (fails)
//
// 2. UPDATE_ROLLBACK_IN_PROGRESS
//    CloudFormation automatically attempts to ROLL BACK all changes
//    RDS instance type reverted (db.t3.small → db.t3.micro)
//    Lambda function (since it failed, nothing to undo)
//
// 3. UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE
//    Stack is back to the state BEFORE the update attempt
//    RDS is back to db.t3.micro (change was reversed)
//    No Lambda function created
//
// WHAT HAPPENED TO THE RDS CHANGE:
//   REVERSED — CloudFormation rolled back the successful RDS change
//   Your RDS is back to db.t3.micro after the rollback completes
//   The entire update is atomic at the stack level
//
// POSSIBLE FAILURE SCENARIO — UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED:
//   If the ROLLBACK itself fails (e.g., RDS modification blocked by a dependency)
//   Stack enters UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED state — stack is stuck
//   Recovery options:
//   a) ContinueUpdateRollback: retry the rollback (fix the blocking issue first)
//   b) Skip specific resources: exclude the failing resource from rollback
//      (resource is removed from stack management — not deleted)
//   c) Manual fix: fix the resource outside CloudFormation, then ContinueUpdateRollback
//
// CREATE vs UPDATE STACK BEHAVIOR:
// CREATE failure:
//   - All resources created during this attempt are DELETED
//   - Stack enters CREATE_FAILED state
//   - Can disable rollback on failure (--disable-rollback) for debugging
//
// UPDATE failure:
//   - Changes made during update are rolled back
//   - Stack returns to PREVIOUS STABLE state (UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE)
//   - Previous resources still exist (RDS reverts, not deleted)
//
// DEBUGGING TIPS:
//   Check CloudFormation Events tab: shows each resource operation and error
//   Disable rollback on failure (preview option): keep failed state for debugging
//   Use --rollback-configuration to customize rollback triggers (CloudWatch alarms)
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Stack update failure → automatic rollback of all update changes
// - UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE: back to pre-update state (RDS reverted)
// - UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED: stack stuck, manual intervention needed
// - ContinueUpdateRollback: resume a stuck rollback
// - CloudFormation Events: tells you exactly which resource failed and why
// - Stack DRIFT: resources manually changed outside CloudFormation (detect with drift detection)

starterCode:
// Four deployment scenarios:
// 1. Test environment: deploy fast, some downtime OK
// 2. Production web tier: zero downtime, gradual shift, auto-rollback
// 3. Production API: OK to run at reduced capacity briefly, gradual shift
// 4. Lambda: test 10% traffic for 10 minutes, then 100%
//
// CodeDeploy deployment strategies:
// - All-at-once
// - Rolling
// - Rolling with additional batch
// - Blue/Green
// - Canary (for Lambda/ECS)
// - Linear (for Lambda/ECS)
//
// Question: Which strategy maps to which scenario? Why?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// SCENARIO 1 (Test environment, fast, some downtime OK):
//   → ALL-AT-ONCE
//   - Deploys to ALL instances simultaneously
//   - Fastest deployment: no waiting for batches
//   - DOWNTIME: all instances down simultaneously during deployment
//   - Failed deployment: ALL instances affected
//   - Rollback: redeploy to all instances again
//
// SCENARIO 3 (Production API, reduced capacity OK, gradual shift):
//   → ROLLING
//   - Deploys to one batch of instances at a time
//   - While batch is being updated: those instances are taken out of service
//   - Capacity REDUCED during deployment (e.g., 75% capacity during batch update)
//   - No downtime but capacity reduction
//   - Failed deployment: some instances on old code, some on new
//   - Safer than All-at-once; slower
//
// VARIATION: ROLLING WITH ADDITIONAL BATCH
//   - Provisions an EXTRA batch of new instances first
//   - Deploys new code to extra batch → adds to load balancer
//   - Then rolls remaining instances
//   - FULL CAPACITY maintained throughout (extra instances cover reduction)
//   - Most expensive (extra instances during deployment)
//
// SCENARIO 2 (Production web tier, zero downtime, auto-rollback):
//   → BLUE/GREEN
//   - Provisions new environment (GREEN) with same capacity as current (BLUE)
//   - Deploys to GREEN, registers with load balancer
//   - Shifts traffic from BLUE to GREEN (configurable: all at once or gradual)
//   - BLUE remains live for configurable wait period (instant rollback available)
//   - Auto-rollback: CloudWatch alarms can trigger automatic rollback
//   - Fastest rollback: traffic switch = seconds (BLUE already warm)
//
// SCENARIO 4 (Lambda, 10% for 10 minutes then 100%):
//   → CANARY (Lambda/ECS specific)
//   - Lambda version/alias based traffic shifting
//   - Canary10Percent10Minutes:
//     * 10% traffic → new version immediately
//     * Wait 10 minutes, monitor CloudWatch alarms
//     * If alarms trigger: roll back to 100% old version
//     * If no alarms: shift 100% to new version
//   - Linear: gradually increase %, e.g., Linear10PercentEvery1Minute
//     * 10% at minute 1, 20% at minute 2, ... 100% at minute 10
//
// DEPLOYMENT STRATEGY SUMMARY:
// All-at-once:             Fast, downtime, test/dev environments
// Rolling:                 Gradual, reduced capacity, lower cost than B/G
// Rolling+extra batch:     Gradual, full capacity, more expensive
// Blue/Green (EC2/ECS):    Zero downtime, instant rollback, highest cost
// Canary (Lambda/ECS):     % traffic shift, auto-rollback on alarms
// Linear (Lambda/ECS):     Gradual % increase, auto-rollback on alarms
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - All-at-once: fastest, downtime, dev only
// - Rolling: gradual, reduced capacity window
// - Blue/Green: most resilient, highest cost, instant rollback
// - Canary/Linear: Lambda/ECS only, percentage-based, CloudWatch alarm rollback

starterCode:
// CI/CD Pipeline requirements:
// Trigger: code merged to main branch in GitHub
// Stage 1: Build (compile, unit test, create artifact)
// Stage 2: Deploy to staging (auto, no approval needed)
// Stage 3: Manual approval gate
// Stage 4: Deploy to production (blue/green, auto rollback on failure)
//
// Question: Which AWS services map to each stage?
// How does CodePipeline trigger on a GitHub merge?
// What is a CodeBuild buildspec.yml?
// How is the manual approval stage configured?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// PIPELINE ARCHITECTURE:
// GitHub (main branch) → CodePipeline → CodeBuild → CodeDeploy/ECS/Lambda
//
// STAGE 1: SOURCE
//   Service: GitHub (via CodeStar Connection or GitHub Action)
//   Trigger: webhook on push to main branch
//   CodePipeline detects the change via:
//   - GitHub webhooks (preferred, near-instant trigger)
//   - CodeConnections (new name for CodeStar Connections)
//   Source artifact: zipped repository contents stored in S3 (artifact bucket)
//
// STAGE 2: BUILD
//   Service: CodeBuild
//   buildspec.yml (in repository root):
//     version: 0.2
//     phases:
//       install:     install dependencies (npm install, pip install)
//       pre_build:   prepare (authentication, code quality checks)
//       build:       compile code (npm build, mvn package)
//       post_build:  run tests, create deployable artifact
//     artifacts:
//       files: ['**/*']  or specific build output
//   Output: build artifact stored in S3 (passed to next stages)
//   Reports: test results, code coverage (visible in CodeBuild console)
//   Environment: managed AWS Linux image or custom Docker image
//
// STAGE 3: DEPLOY TO STAGING
//   Service: CodeDeploy (for EC2/ECS) or ECS Deploy Action
//   Auto-triggered (no approval)
//   Deploy build artifact to staging environment
//   Run integration/smoke tests against staging
//
// STAGE 4: MANUAL APPROVAL
//   Service: CodePipeline built-in 'Manual Approval' action
//   Config: approval message, URL to review (e.g., staging URL), SNS topic
//   SNS notification: email to approvers with approval link
//   Pipeline PAUSES: waits for Approve or Reject (up to 7 days)
//   Approve: pipeline continues to production deploy
//   Reject: pipeline stops (no production deployment)
//
// STAGE 5: DEPLOY TO PRODUCTION
//   Service: CodeDeploy (Blue/Green strategy)
//   Same artifact deployed to production ALB/ASG
//   Blue/Green: zero downtime, wait period for rollback window
//   CloudWatch alarms configured for auto-rollback
//
// KEY PIPELINE CONCEPTS:
//   Artifact bucket: S3 bucket where pipeline passes artifacts between stages
//   CodePipeline role: IAM role that pipeline uses to access all services
//   Stage Actions: each stage can have multiple parallel actions
//   Pipeline approval emails: sent via SNS when approval action reached
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - CodePipeline: orchestrates stages (Source → Build → Test → Deploy)
// - CodeBuild: executes build instructions from buildspec.yml
// - CodeDeploy: handles actual deployment (rolling, blue/green, canary)
// - Manual Approval: pipeline pauses, SNS notification, 7-day max wait
// - Artifacts: passed via S3 between pipeline stages
// - The full suite: CodeCommit/GitHub + CodeBuild + CodeDeploy + CodePipeline = full CI/CD

starterCode:
// Three applications needing the right compute:
// 1. Legacy PHP app: small team, no deep AWS expertise, just upload code and run
// 2. 15-service containerized microservices: need independent scaling, networking
// 3. Event-driven transformations: <5 min, triggered by S3, variable frequency
//
// Options:
// - Elastic Beanstalk (managed platform)
// - Amazon ECS on Fargate (managed containers)
// - AWS Lambda (serverless functions)
//
// Question: Which is best for each? What are the key tradeoffs?
// What does each service manage vs what you manage?
// What is the underlying infrastructure for each?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// APPLICATION 1 (Legacy PHP, small team): → ELASTIC BEANSTALK
//   What it manages FOR YOU:
//   - EC2 instances (creates and manages the fleet)
//   - Load balancer (ALB automatically created)
//   - Auto Scaling group
//   - OS patching (managed platform updates)
//   - Deployment health monitoring
//   What YOU control:
//   - Application code (just upload ZIP or connect Git)
//   - Environment config (instance type, scaling rules)
//   - Platform selection (PHP 8.1, Node.js, Python, etc.)
//   Access: still have full access to underlying EC2 if needed
//   Platforms: PHP, Node.js, Python, Ruby, Go, Docker, Java/Tomcat
//   Deployment: upload code → Beanstalk handles the rest
//   Best for: lift-and-shift, small teams without deep AWS knowledge
//
// APPLICATION 2 (15 containerized microservices): → ECS on FARGATE
//   What it manages FOR YOU:
//   - Underlying EC2 infrastructure (Fargate = serverless containers)
//   - Container runtime management
//   What YOU control:
//   - Task definitions (container image, CPU/memory, env vars, ports)
//   - Service definitions (desired count, load balancer, auto scaling)
//   - VPC networking (subnets, security groups per service)
//   - Service discovery (Cloud Map)
//   - Deployment strategies (rolling, blue/green via CodeDeploy)
//   Full control: each service can scale independently
//   Best for: containerized apps needing fine-grained control, microservices
//
// APPLICATION 3 (Event-driven, S3-triggered, <5 min): → LAMBDA
//   What it manages FOR YOU:
//   - ALL infrastructure (servers, OS, runtime, scaling)
//   - Automatic scaling: 0 to 10,000s of concurrent executions
//   What YOU control:
//   - Function code + dependencies
//   - Memory (128 MB - 10 GB)
//   - Timeout (1 sec - 15 min)
//   - Trigger configuration (S3 event → Lambda)
//   Cost: pay ONLY for execution time (no charge when idle)
//   Best for: event-driven, variable/unpredictable workloads, short duration
//
// MANAGEMENT RESPONSIBILITY COMPARISON:
//                 | Lambda | Fargate | Beanstalk | EC2
// OS/Runtime      | AWS    | AWS     | AWS       | YOU
// Container mgmt  | N/A    | AWS     | AWS(Docker)| YOU
// App deployment  | YOU    | YOU     | YOU       | YOU
// Scaling config  | Auto   | YOU     | Managed   | YOU
// Infrastructure  | AWS    | AWS     | AWS       | YOU
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Beanstalk: PaaS, fastest to deploy, still EC2 underneath, good for lift-and-shift
// - ECS/Fargate: containers, fine-grained control, microservices
// - Lambda: serverless, event-driven, pay per use, 15-min max
// - Beanstalk ≠ fully managed (you still choose environment, config)
// - ECS EC2 launch type: you manage EC2 cluster; Fargate: AWS manages servers

starterCode:
// Security baseline must be deployed to:
// - 50 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations
// - 3 regions per account (150 stack instances total)
//
// Requirements:
// - Deploy to all accounts/regions from one central location
// - Individual account admins cannot delete security resources
// - Updates propagate to all accounts automatically
//
// Question: What is CloudFormation StackSets?
// What is a StackSet vs a Stack instance?
// How does StackSets integrate with AWS Organizations?
// What is the 'self-managed' vs 'service-managed' permissions model?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// CLOUDFORMATION STACKSETS:
//   - Deploy ONE CloudFormation template to MULTIPLE accounts and/or regions
//   - Manage from a single administrator account (management account or delegated)
//   - StackSet: the template + deployment configuration
//   - Stack Instances: individual stacks created in each target account/region pair
//   - 50 accounts × 3 regions = 150 stack instances from 1 StackSet
//
// PERMISSIONS MODELS:
//
// SELF-MANAGED (original model):
//   - Manually create IAM roles in each target account:
//     * AWSCloudFormationStackSetAdministrationRole (in admin account)
//     * AWSCloudFormationStackSetExecutionRole (in each target account)
//   - Admin account assumes execution role in target accounts
//   - Does NOT automatically include new accounts (manual addition required)
//   - Use for: cross-account deployments without AWS Organizations
//
// SERVICE-MANAGED (integrates with AWS Organizations):
//   - AWS Organizations handles IAM role creation automatically
//   - Deploy to entire OUs or root (all accounts in organization)
//   - AUTO-DEPLOYMENT: new accounts joining the OU automatically get stack instance
//   - ACCOUNT REMOVAL: when account leaves OU, stack instance can be auto-deleted
//   - Management account or delegated admin account can create StackSets
//   → RECOMMENDED for this scenario (50 accounts, new accounts expected)
//
// PROTECTING RESOURCES FROM LOCAL DELETION:
//   - Stack Instances in target accounts have 'stack instance status'
//   - Individual account admins CAN see the stack in their account
//   - Stack deletion protection: enable 'Prevent stack deletion' on stack instances
//   - SCPs: create SCP that DENIES cloudformation:DeleteStack on specific stack names
//     → Even admins in member accounts cannot delete security baseline stacks
//
// UPDATING ALL ACCOUNTS:
//   - Update StackSet template → CloudFormation deploys to all instances
//   - Concurrency controls:
//     * Max concurrent accounts: N accounts updated simultaneously
//     * Failure tolerance: allowed failures before rollback
//   - Update order: accounts sorted alphabetically or by OU
//   - Each instance rolled back independently if its update fails
//
// DEPLOYMENT EXAMPLE:
//   StackSet: SecurityBaseline
//   Template: CloudTrail + Config + GuardDuty + Security Hub
//   Deployment targets: Root OU (all 50 accounts)
//   Regions: us-east-1, us-west-2, eu-west-1
//   Result: 150 stack instances deployed and maintained centrally
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - StackSets: one template → multiple accounts/regions
// - Stack instance: individual stack in one account/region pair
// - Service-managed: AWS Organizations integration, auto-deploy to new accounts
// - Self-managed: manual IAM roles, no auto-deployment
// - SCPs + StackSets: combination prevents local modification/deletion
// - Delegated admin: non-management account can administer StackSets

starterCode:
// Lambda function: ~2% failure rate
// Failure types: timeouts, DynamoDB throttling, unknown errors
// Current observability: none (just error count metric)
//
// Question: What CloudWatch features help diagnose Lambda failures?
// How does X-Ray help identify performance bottlenecks?
// How do you write CloudWatch Logs Insights queries?
// What metrics should you alarm on?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// THREE-LAYER OBSERVABILITY APPROACH:
//
// LAYER 1: CLOUDWATCH METRICS (What is happening)
//   Lambda automatically publishes:
//   - Errors: count of invocations that resulted in an error
//   - Throttles: count of throttled invocations
//   - Duration (avg, p95, p99): execution time percentiles
//   - ConcurrentExecutions: simultaneous invocations
//   - IteratorAge (for Kinesis/DynamoDB Streams): lag behind tip of stream
//
//   ALARMS TO CREATE:
//   - Error rate > 1% → SNS alert to PagerDuty/Slack
//   - Duration P99 > 80% of timeout → latency warning
//   - Throttles > 0 → scaling issue
//   - Composite Alarm: Errors AND Throttles → differentiated alerting
//
// LAYER 2: CLOUDWATCH LOGS INSIGHTS (Why is it happening)
//   Query Lambda log group: /aws/lambda/function-name
//
//   Find all error messages:
//   fields @timestamp, @message
//   | filter @message like /ERROR/
//   | sort @timestamp desc
//   | limit 100
//
//   Analyze error types:
//   fields @message
//   | parse @message 'ERROR * *' as errorType, errorDetail
//   | stats count(*) by errorType
//   | sort count desc
//
//   Find slow invocations (timeout candidates):
//   filter @type = 'REPORT'
//   | stats max(@duration), avg(@duration), count(*)
//   | filter @duration > 10000  (>10 seconds)
//
// LAYER 3: AWS X-RAY (Where is it slow)
//   Enable X-Ray on Lambda function (active tracing)
//   Lambda SDK instruments: each downstream call creates a trace segment:
//   - Lambda invocation → X-Ray trace
//     └── DynamoDB GetItem → subsegment (duration: 45ms)
//     └── DynamoDB PutItem → subsegment (duration: 3200ms ← BOTTLENECK)
//     └── SQS SendMessage → subsegment (duration: 12ms)
//
//   X-Ray Service Map: visual graph of all services + error rates + latency
//   X-Ray Analytics: filter traces by error, throttle, fault, latency percentiles
//   Find specific trace: filter Throttled = true on DynamoDB subsegments
//     → Identifies DynamoDB throttling as root cause
//
// CLOUDWATCH LAMBDA INSIGHTS (enhanced metrics):
//   Lambda Insights extension: memory usage, CPU utilization per invocation
//   Enable as Lambda Layer (CloudWatch Lambda Insights extension)
//   Provides: memory utilization %, CPU usage, function duration histogram
//
// INVESTIGATION WORKFLOW:
//   1. Check CloudWatch metrics: 2% error rate confirmed, mix of Errors + Throttles
//   2. Logs Insights: query for ERROR messages → 60% DynamoDB ProvisionedThroughputExceeded
//   3. X-Ray trace map: DynamoDB GetItem subsegments show throttling
//   4. Root cause: DynamoDB table under-provisioned, not Lambda code issue
//   5. Fix: increase DynamoDB RCU/WCU or switch to On-Demand mode
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - CloudWatch Metrics: numerical data (error counts, duration)
// - CloudWatch Logs Insights: text search and analysis of log data
// - X-Ray: distributed tracing, service map, latency analysis
// - Lambda structured logging (JSON): makes Logs Insights queries more powerful
// - Lambda Powertools: SDK for structured logging, custom X-Ray subsegments, metrics

starterCode:
// Alert requirements:
// 1. Alert ONLY when BOTH CPU > 80% AND Memory > 80% simultaneously
//    (not when just one is high — that's normal)
// 2. Alert when 4XX error rate is abnormally high relative to historical pattern
//    (traffic varies by hour, so fixed threshold would create false positives)
//
// Question: What are CloudWatch Composite Alarms?
// What is CloudWatch Anomaly Detection?
// What is the difference between CloudWatch Alarms and Composite Alarms?
// How does anomaly detection work?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// REQUIREMENT 1: COMPOSITE ALARM (CPU AND Memory)
//
// STEP 1: Create individual alarms:
//   Alarm A: CPUUtilization > 80% for 5 consecutive minutes
//   Alarm B: MemoryUtilization > 80% for 5 consecutive minutes
//   (Memory: requires CloudWatch Agent — not built-in EC2 metric)
//
// STEP 2: Create Composite Alarm:
//   CompositeAlarm: ALARM(CpuHighAlarm) AND ALARM(MemoryHighAlarm)
//   → Alarm fires ONLY when both Alarm A AND Alarm B are in ALARM state
//   → When only CPU is high: no alert (Composite alarm stays OK)
//   → When only Memory is high: no alert (Composite alarm stays OK)
//   → When BOTH are high simultaneously: Composite fires → SNS notification
//
// COMPOSITE ALARM BENEFITS:
//   - Reduces alert fatigue: compound conditions
//   - Boolean logic: AND, OR, NOT supported
//   - Example: (CPU OR Memory) AND NOT (planned maintenance)
//   - Can combine alarms from different metrics, accounts, regions
//   - Individual alarms can still have their own actions (warn without page)
//   - Composite alarm has its own SNS actions (page the on-call)
//
// REQUIREMENT 2: ANOMALY DETECTION (4XX error rate)
//
// CloudWatch Anomaly Detection:
//   - ML model trained on metric's historical data
//   - Learns patterns: daily cycles, weekly trends, seasonal variation
//   - Creates a dynamic BAND of expected values
//   - Band = upper and lower bounds that represent 'normal'
//   - Alert when metric goes OUTSIDE the band (anomaly)
//   - Sensitivity (standard deviations): 1=tight band, 2=medium, 3=loose band
//
// ANOMALY DETECTION ALARM SETUP:
//   Metric: 4XXError count from ALB
//   Comparison: GreaterThanUpperThreshold (above the anomaly band)
//   Band width: 2 standard deviations (anomaly at 95% confidence)
//   At 2 AM when traffic is normally 100 req/min: band might be [50-150]
//   At 2 PM when traffic is normally 5000 req/min: band might be [3000-7000]
//   → Fixed threshold (e.g., 500 errors) would false-alarm during peak
//   → Anomaly Detection adapts the threshold to time of day automatically
//
// CLOUDWATCH AGENT (for memory metrics):
//   - Install CloudWatch Agent on EC2 instances
//   - Config: collect MemoryUsage%, disk usage, and custom application metrics
//   - Metrics published to CloudWatch under custom namespace (CWAgent/)
//   - IAM role for EC2 must have CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Composite Alarms: boolean logic (AND/OR/NOT) on other alarms
// - Anomaly Detection: ML-based dynamic threshold, adapts to patterns
// - Memory is NOT a default EC2 metric — requires CloudWatch Agent
// - Composite Alarms reduce alert fatigue by requiring multiple conditions
// - Anomaly Detection band width: narrow (1σ) = more alerts; wide (3σ) = fewer

