// KMS key management questions:
// 1. How does automatic key rotation work? Are old ciphertexts still readable?
// 2. A developer accidentally created and used a KMS key that should be deleted.
//    How do you delete a KMS key? What is the minimum waiting period?
// 3. Who controls access to a KMS key — the key policy or IAM policy?
// 4. What is the difference between AWS-managed keys and customer-managed keys?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
//
// QUESTION 1: AUTOMATIC KEY ROTATION
//   Enable: AWS KMS Console → Customer Managed Key → Key Rotation → Enable automatic rotation
//   Rotation period: every 365 days (1 year) — automatic
//   Custom period: 90-2560 days (newer feature)
//
//   HOW ROTATION WORKS:
//   - KMS generates NEW backing key material
//   - Key ID, ARN, aliases: UNCHANGED (transparent to applications)
//   - Old key material: PRESERVED (kept for decryption of old ciphertexts)
//   - New encryptions: use the new key material
//   - Old ciphertexts: automatically decrypted using the key material version they were encrypted with
//   - Applications: NO CODE CHANGES needed — same key ARN works for all versions
//
//   WHAT ROTATION DOES NOT DO:
//   - Does NOT delete old key material (needed for old ciphertexts)
//   - Does NOT require re-encryption of existing data (AWS handles it transparently)
//   - Does NOT change the key ARN or aliases
//
// QUESTION 2: KMS KEY DELETION
//   CANNOT delete immediately — must schedule deletion
//   Waiting period: 7 to 30 days (you choose, minimum 7 days)
//   During waiting period: key status = 'Pending deletion' (cannot use for encryption/decryption)
//   Cancel deletion: possible during waiting period (before permanent deletion)
//   After waiting period: key permanently deleted (IRREVERSIBLE)
//
//   CONSEQUENCES OF KEY DELETION:
//   - All data encrypted with the deleted key becomes PERMANENTLY INACCESSIBLE
//   - No way to recover encrypted data after key is deleted
//   - Best practice: set up CloudWatch alarm for KMS key usage during pending deletion
//     → If alarm fires (data was being accessed), cancel deletion immediately
//
// QUESTION 3: KEY POLICY vs IAM POLICY
//   KMS KEY POLICY is the PRIMARY control:
//   - Every KMS key has a resource-based key policy
//   - Default key policy: 'Allow root account to manage key, allow IAM users'
//   - If key policy does NOT allow IAM to control the key, IAM policies are IGNORED
//   - BOTH key policy AND IAM policy must allow an action for it to succeed
//
//   KEY POLICY REQUIRED ELEMENTS:
//   - Key Administrators: can manage the key (create/delete/rotate)
//   - Key Users: can use the key (encrypt/decrypt) via applications
//   - Root account: must be in key policy or risk losing key access forever
//
// QUESTION 4: AWS-MANAGED vs CUSTOMER-MANAGED KEYS
//   AWS-Managed Keys (aws/service):
//   - Created by AWS services (aws/s3, aws/rds, aws/ebs)
//   - Cannot view or modify key policy
//   - Automatically rotated every 3 years
//   - Free to use; but less control, no cross-account use
//
//   Customer-Managed Keys (CMK):
//   - Created and owned by you
//   - Full control: key policy, rotation schedule, key deletion
//   - Can be used cross-account (share via key policy)
//   - $1/month/key + $0.03/10,000 API calls
//   - Annual rotation configurable
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Rotation: new key material, same ARN, old data still decryptable
// - Deletion: 7-30 day waiting period, irreversible, access to old data lost
// - Key policy: primary access control (must allow IAM if you want IAM to work)
// - AWS-managed keys: free, no control; CMK: $1/month, full control
// - Deleting CMK with data encrypted = permanent data loss

