// Four storage needs:
// 1. DB passwords: auto-rotate every 30 days, encrypted
// 2. API keys: occasional manual rotation, encrypted
// 3. Thousands of config params: non-sensitive, many services read them, cost-conscious
// 4. DB password in Account A: must be accessible by Lambda in Account B
//
// Two options: AWS Secrets Manager vs AWS SSM Parameter Store
//
// Question: What are the key differences between the two services?
// Which is more cost-effective for high-volume non-sensitive config?
// Which supports automatic rotation and cross-account sharing?
//
// Your answer:

solution.code:
// ANSWER:
// COMPARISON:
//
// AWS SECRETS MANAGER:
//   Cost: $0.40/secret/month + $0.05/10,000 API calls
//   Auto-rotation: YES — built-in rotation for RDS, Redshift, DocumentDB;
//                 custom rotation via Lambda for any secret
//   Cross-account: YES — resource-based policy on secret allows other accounts
//   Encryption: ALWAYS encrypted with KMS (required, not optional)
//   Secret size: up to 64 KB per secret value
//   Versions: stores multiple versions (AWSCURRENT, AWSPREVIOUS, AWSPENDING)
//   Use for: passwords, API keys requiring rotation, cross-account secrets
//
// SSM PARAMETER STORE:
//   Cost:
//     Standard tier: FREE (first 10,000 params); API calls: $0.05/10,000
//     Advanced tier: $0.05/param/month; larger size, higher throughput, TTL
//   Auto-rotation: NO (manual rotation or custom automation needed)
//   Cross-account: NO (cannot directly share across accounts)
//   Encryption: Optional — String (plaintext), SecureString (KMS encrypted)
//   Parameter size: 4 KB (standard), 8 KB (advanced)
//   Use for: config values, feature flags, non-sensitive settings, high volume
//
// SOLUTION FOR EACH REQUIREMENT:
//
// Req 1 (DB passwords, auto-rotate 30 days):
//   → AWS Secrets Manager
//   Enable automatic rotation with built-in RDS Lambda blueprint
//   $0.40/month per password — justified by zero operational overhead for rotation
//
// Req 2 (API keys, occasional manual rotation):
//   → AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store SecureString
//   If only occasionally rotated manually: SSM SecureString is cheaper ($0 standard tier)
//   If cross-account sharing needed: must use Secrets Manager
//   Choose based on: rotation frequency vs cost vs cross-account needs
//
// Req 3 (Thousands of config params, non-sensitive):
//   → SSM Parameter Store (Standard tier)
//   Free for up to 10,000 params
//   Cost for 5,000 config params: $0/month vs $2,000/month in Secrets Manager
//   Use String type (no KMS needed for non-sensitive data)
//   SSM GetParametersByPath: retrieve all params under a path prefix
//
// Req 4 (Cross-account DB password sharing):
//   → AWS Secrets Manager (only option with native cross-account support)
//   Secret in Account A with resource-based policy:
//     Principal: arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:role/lambda-execution-role
//     Action: secretsmanager:GetSecretValue
//   Lambda in Account B calls GetSecretValue on Account A's secret ARN
//   Lambda execution role needs: secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on cross-account secret ARN
//
// COST COMPARISON EXAMPLE (1,000 secrets, monthly):
//   Secrets Manager: 1,000 × $0.40 = $400/month
//   SSM Parameter Store (standard): FREE
//   SSM Parameter Store (advanced): 1,000 × $0.05 = $50/month
//
// KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER:
// - Secrets Manager: auto-rotation, cross-account, $0.40/secret/month
// - Parameter Store: free standard tier, no auto-rotation, no cross-account
// - For cost: thousands of configs → SSM; dozens of secrets needing rotation → Secrets Manager
// - Cross-account: ONLY Secrets Manager supports native cross-account sharing
// - Both: can be cached in Lambda global scope (reduce API calls and latency)
